WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.790
- So welcome, everyone.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:12.120
Thank you so much for coming
to this special event.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.197
My name is Hwansoo Kim,

00:00:13.197 --> 00:00:15.600
and I teach Korean religion and culture

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.640
in the Department of Religious Studies.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:22.053
I also serve as chair of the
council on East Asian Study.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.910
It is my tremendous
honor to host this event

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:31.910
for two prominent scholars of
East Asian history, politics,

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:32.973
and culture.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.840
The central theme of today's
conversation is what is Korea?

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:43.530
Of course, Korea has not
existed in isolation,

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.320
and thus, the more specific question is

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.910
how has Korea preserved its
political and cultural identity

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:55.910
throughout the 5,000 years
of its tumultuous history?

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.190
An identity that it has
shared, and negotiated,

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:02.490
and contested with those of
its neighboring countries,

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:06.420
China, and Japan, and beyond.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.350
Professor Myung Chul
Youn will give two talks.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.500
One today, and the other, this Friday,

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.753
to shed some lights on this subject.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:20.610
And to make our
conversations more relevant

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.730
to modern and contemporary
international relations,

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.720
Professor Odd Arne Westad

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.520
from the Department of
History here at the Yale

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.220
is kindly joining us to share reflections

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.520
from his most and highly acclaimed book

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.120
called "Empire and Righteous Nations,

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.650
600 Years of China-Korea Relations,"

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:46.053
which is also translated into Korean.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.820
So these three talks
combined, luckily for us,

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:55.530
will spend the entire 5,000
years of Korean history,

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:58.350
and enlighten us on the distinctive

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.620
and common characteristics
of Korean identity

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.680
in the regional and global context.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.430
These talks will also eliminate
the precarious conditions

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.980
on the Korean Peninsula in the face

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:13.830
of a Beijing centric order,

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:16.950
and the increasing assertiveness of Korea

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:20.010
with this international influence.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:23.220
So today's event will be
divided into two parts.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:26.100
Professor Westad will
first speak for 30 minutes

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.073
about his book, followed
by Professor Youn.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:32.250
Professor Youn's presentation
will be translated

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.280
by my dear colleague, Professor Juhn Ahn

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.380
from the University of Michigan.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.213
Thank you so much.

00:02:38.213 --> 00:02:40.503
He's a professor of Korean studies.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.723
So he's joining us,
especially for this purpose.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.660
After the two presentations,

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.640
our speakers will have the opportunity

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.460
to ask questions of each other,

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.850
and we'll then open the
floor for your questions

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:57.993
from the audience.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.400
In the interest of time,

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:06.480
and due to the video
recording of today's event,

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.630
I would ask that you write your questions

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:12.180
on the note cards being distributed,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.330
and I'll be reading later
on after collecting them.

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:19.080
Let me briefly introduce our two speakers.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.970
Dr. Myung Chul Youn is a
Professor Emeritus of History

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.970
at Dongguk University,
and is currently Professor

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:31.683
of Archeology at Samarkand
State University of Uzbekistan.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:36.630
His research focuses on the
history of the Korea period

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:40.320
from the first century BC
to the eighth century CE,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.203
as well as East Asian Maritime history.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:48.450
He's published over 60 books
and numerous journal articles.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.850
He's also poet, an explorer,

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.313
and has published 14 books of poetry.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.840
Professor Odd Arne Westad is a scholar

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.870
of modern international and global history

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:04.590
with a specialization in
the history of Eastern Asia

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:07.380
from the 18th century on.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.570
He studied at the University of Oslo

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:11.820
before receiving his graduate degree

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.240
at the University of North
Carolina in Chapel Hill.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.550
Professor Westad has published 16 books,

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:20.790
many articles, and
received numerous awards.

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.830
He taught at the London
School of Economics

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:29.750
and Harvard History, and you
joined Yale three years ago?

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:33.030
- 2019.
- Yeah.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:37.470
And Yale snatched him
from Harvard. (laughs)

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:40.050
Today's event is supported
by Hong-nam Kim's

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:41.970
Korean Studies Fund.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:46.110
And special thanks go to
the council staff, Kathy,

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:51.110
and particularly in Jung
Kim, without logistic helps,

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:56.760
I will be deeply exhausted
and sick in bed. (laughs)

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:58.080
So without further ado,

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.563
please join me in welcoming
Professor Arne Westad.

00:05:01.563 --> 00:05:04.730
(audience applauding)

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:09.960
- So thank you very much, Hwansoo,

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:11.790
for that wonderful introduction.

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:14.730
It's a great honor for me
today to speak together

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:17.130
with Professor Youn.

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:18.870
I want to hear what he has to say.

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:21.960
So I will be relatively
brief in presenting my book.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.450
And for those of you who are at Yale,

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:29.450
you know where to find me, so
you can easily seek me out,

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:31.230
and discuss these things with me.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:36.230
So this is a book that came
out of a set of lectures,

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:40.833
so-called Reischauer Lectures
that I did at Harvard in 2017.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:46.680
And the idea is to try to
look at the relationship

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.680
between China and Korea over
a long period of time, right?

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:57.785
And in order to get to some
of the essential aspects

00:05:57.785 --> 00:05:59.190
of that relationship.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.020
It has been an ever-changing relationship,

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:03.630
as you'll hear me talk about in a minute.

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:08.630
But there are some structural
aspects of it that seem to me

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:12.330
to connect at least the
period when I start up

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:17.130
at the beginning of the
Ming and the Joseon states

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:19.830
are two waters come later.

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:24.180
So it is not without relevance
when we try to understand

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:28.860
today's relationship between
the new Chinese state

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:31.639
calls itself the People's
Republic of China

00:06:31.639 --> 00:06:34.620
and the two Korean states.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:35.850
So history matters,

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:39.180
but not always in the
manner that we assume.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.240
There is no automaticity in this.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:44.070
But people learn from their history,

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.470
at least they try to
learn from their history.

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:47.613
Because there isn't all that much more

00:06:47.613 --> 00:06:49.020
that you can learn from,

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:51.420
particularly when you're
dealing with neighbors from.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:54.390
In Korea's case, very powerful neighbors

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:59.097
to the north, and the
western, and the east as well.

00:06:59.097 --> 00:07:00.447
You have to learn.

00:07:00.447 --> 00:07:04.140
And Korea has learned
through that long process.

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:06.360
The empires learn as well.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:07.767
Though they learn differently.

00:07:07.767 --> 00:07:10.284
They learn in a different form.

00:07:10.284 --> 00:07:14.760
This is where the title
of the book comes from,

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:17.673
empire and righteous nation.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:22.470
And Professor Youn has the
recently published Korean edition

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:25.743
to which I wrote a new preface.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:31.713
So by talking about empire,

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:35.970
it wouldn't be that
difficult to understand

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:39.300
who I have in mind when
I speak about empire.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:41.430
I speak about China in its various forms.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:44.880
So we are dealing with
several different incarnations

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:49.717
of these states, going
back to the early Ming era.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.460
I wanna talk a little bit
about empire in a minute

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:54.510
in more general terms,

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:57.990
but it's pretty clear what
the title refers to, at least,

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:59.310
in terms of empire.

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:02.793
Now, righteous and nation are
more complicated concepts.

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:06.990
And I spend quite a bit of time

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:11.990
at the beginning of this book
to talk about those concepts.

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:16.140
Now, by referring to
Korea, again, no surprise,

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:19.680
it's Korea referring to
when I use the expression

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:22.620
righteous nation as righteous.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:25.300
I don't of course mean that
every Korean is righteous

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:27.503
far from it.

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:30.993
That is not based on historical evidence.

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:34.006
I like Koreans, but not that much,

00:08:34.006 --> 00:08:35.670
that I would call all of them righteous.

00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:38.463
It would also be unfair
too much to live up to.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:43.380
What I mean is that
concepts of righteousness.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:47.490
E in Chinese, we in Korean is something

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:49.860
that has played a particular role

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:52.173
in Korean history going far back.

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:57.750
It is an integral part of
the Neo-Confucian revolution

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:02.750
that the early Joseon state
signaled and symbolized.

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:07.740
And it also became a key aspect,

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:12.300
both of trying to understand
how Korean society should work,

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:16.950
but perhaps more importantly
over time at critical junctures

00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:19.803
in Korean history, as you
will know better than me,

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:22.713
as a resistance to foreign domination.

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:26.520
So righteousness in this context,

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.110
of course, has many meanings.

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:32.160
But part of it is an appeal
to a Korean understanding

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:35.190
of the fundaments of what
it means to be Korean.

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:37.140
The essence of the discussion

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:38.640
that we are going to have today,

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.290
which of course has varied
enormously over time, right?

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:43.530
It is not in any way static.

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:47.280
But this concept seems to me
to come up again and again

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:48.513
in various forms.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:52.440
Now, the concept nation is of course,

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:57.030
also if you go back to
the late 14th century

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:59.610
problematic with regard to Korea.

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:03.000
And I'd like us to spend some
time if we have afterwards,

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.023
maybe discussing.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.337
As I was writing this book,

00:10:08.337 --> 00:10:11.790
and I actually wrote most
of the book in Beijing

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:15.813
when I was visiting at Peking University.

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:18.810
I started thinking very seriously

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:20.880
about the use of the concept nation,

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:24.663
which of course is a 19th century
European invention, right?

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:31.220
In order to try to
understand Korean history

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:36.550
in an earlier time period,
if you use this concept

00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:40.320
within a framework of automaticity,

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:42.570
of course it becomes
centrally anachronistic.

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:48.930
But if you, as Professor
Habush and others, have done

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:53.850
go to the core of the
European romantic concept

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:57.450
of nation in terms of how it is defined.

00:10:57.450 --> 00:11:00.960
Then I think it's very
hard to avoid the tool

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:05.853
that Korea very distinctly
in an eastern nation context.

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:10.950
Have some of those
characteristics already coming out

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:14.850
of the beginning or being
formed during the beginning

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:17.073
of the time period that I'm talking about.

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:20.820
And particularly during
the Great East Asian War

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:25.020
in the late 16th century,
as has been noted

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:27.570
by many historians that I build on,

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.523
not these Korean historians.

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:33.940
Some of those characteristics
of what it meant to be

00:11:35.109 --> 00:11:37.530
in a professed proud way,

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:40.080
a nation in Europe in the 19th century

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:43.200
also seem to be rather strikingly similar.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:47.100
To some, not all, of the
concepts that Korean identity

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:49.623
builds during that time.

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:53.490
So that's why it's called
"Empire and Righteous Nation."

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:55.500
That's a long explanation for a title,

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:58.290
but it is one that really
brings you, I think,

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:03.000
to the core of what I want
to discuss in this book

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:06.120
about what states relatively intact

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:08.013
and what changes over time.

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:13.483
So the book is three parts to it.

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:16.980
My students, when I was writing it,

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:21.980
like to joke that the
first part deals with about

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:28.800
500 years of Sino-Korean relations.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:33.000
And the second part deals
with maybe 70, 80 years

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:34.167
of Sino-Korean relations.

00:12:34.167 --> 00:12:36.970
And the third part deals
with the last three weeks

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:39.330
of Sino-Korean relations.

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:41.580
It's not quite as bad as that,

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:46.580
but the first part is very
much about the relationship

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:52.413
between two empires based in
China, the Ming and the Ching,

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:57.120
and the Joseon State that both
of them had to interact with,

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:01.893
going up to the late 19th century.

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:06.660
The second part, which I
call the internationalization

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:10.590
of East Asia, China, Korea, and the world

00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:14.103
goes from the 1860s, 1870s, up to 1992.

00:13:16.317 --> 00:13:19.530
And then the third one
deals with the period

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:21.630
much more recently and
particularly the future

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:24.540
of Sino-Korean relations
in the 21st century.

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:28.890
So that is how the book is constructed.

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:32.340
Let me talk just very
briefly about each of these

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:36.990
three parts before I pass the
floor over to Professor Kim,

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:38.133
and then perhaps you can talk about

00:13:38.133 --> 00:13:39.930
some of these afterwards.

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:44.930
So I said that both Ming
and Joseon were created

00:13:48.927 --> 00:13:51.180
in a very similar time period

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:53.130
at the end of the 14th century,

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:57.900
very much under the influence
of the same points of ideas

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:02.900
about state and the
construction of society,

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:04.750
the reconstruction of society

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:08.343
at the end of the 14th century.

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:13.860
In both cases, as historians have shown,

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:17.103
you could talk about a
Neo-Confucian revolution.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:23.080
It's particularly striking
in the Joseon project,

00:14:23.080 --> 00:14:28.080
because of the way the regime
in spite of its own institutes

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:33.300
and difficulties with getting established,

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:35.010
as many of you will know about,

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:39.390
insisted on the Neo-Confucian framework

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:41.320
as being the foundation of the state

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:45.033
in a very direct, rather concrete way.

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:49.137
The Ming, which by the
way, as many of you know,

00:14:49.137 --> 00:14:52.830
had the same problems
with getting the empire,

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:54.573
the dynasty established,

00:14:55.590 --> 00:15:00.150
also used forms of Neo-Confucian thinking

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:02.583
in order to establish that state.

00:15:04.290 --> 00:15:06.540
I don't have time to
go into the differences

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:07.373
between the two.

00:15:07.373 --> 00:15:09.210
There were significant differences.

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:12.090
But there were also
significant similarities.

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:14.920
And one of the trends that I follow then

00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:21.110
for the 15th and the
16th century especially,

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:25.560
is how various ideas and
concepts on the Korean

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:30.270
and on the Chinese side
mutually strengthen,

00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:34.860
reinforce each other during
a long-term conversation

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:36.990
among your Neo-Confucian
scholars that go on

00:15:36.990 --> 00:15:38.400
during those two centuries,

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:43.383
or at least up to the middle
part of the 16th century.

00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:46.830
I find that hugely interesting,

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:50.370
because it is one of
those cases where you find

00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:52.579
that there is a culture.

00:15:52.579 --> 00:15:54.180
And it's right to call
this a culture, I think.

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:55.950
Even though it's more limited

00:15:55.950 --> 00:15:58.250
than many other forms
of culture that we know.

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:04.230
Where you have people from
very different backgrounds

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:06.180
speaking different languages,

00:16:06.180 --> 00:16:07.680
but of course expressing themselves

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:10.770
in written form in similar terms.

00:16:10.770 --> 00:16:13.642
Exchanging ideas about how you can build

00:16:13.642 --> 00:16:16.073
and just write your state.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:19.830
And also crucially about
how the relationship

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:21.993
between the two should work.

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:26.040
And that is where the issue that I deal

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:27.180
with more than anything else

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:29.760
in the first chapter really comes too,

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:34.760
because it is one thing to
proclaim Confucian righteousness.

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:37.517
It's another thing to
try to implement this

00:16:37.517 --> 00:16:40.440
in the relationship between states.

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:41.880
And that's of course something

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:44.610
that the Sino- Korean
relationship has always

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:47.553
had trouble with defining.

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:53.340
So what I find is that in
spite of this flourishing world

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:58.000
of exchange of ideas, and
for that matter also of goods

00:16:58.950 --> 00:17:03.112
that existed between China and Korea,

00:17:03.112 --> 00:17:06.480
the Joseon insistence on being linked

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:11.370
but outside the empire,
is perhaps the core value

00:17:11.370 --> 00:17:14.493
that regulates the
relationship between the two.

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:19.500
And this is important, because
the main in other forms,

00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:21.330
as many of you will know,

00:17:21.330 --> 00:17:23.730
and of course the Ching
even more than followed it,

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:26.100
were expansionist states, right?

00:17:26.100 --> 00:17:28.380
It wasn't given from the beginning,

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:31.143
particularly with this
definition of a common culture,

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:36.810
that the Joseon state would
necessarily stay outside

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:39.270
of these expanding empires,

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:41.673
and particularly through for the Ching.

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:45.120
So in addition to the principles

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:47.370
that both of them based themselves on,

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:50.910
there also had to be a
lot of practical thinking

00:17:50.910 --> 00:17:54.210
about how this relationship
should actually work,

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:56.100
and how it should be expressed.

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:58.350
Because within the
Neo-Confucian framework,

00:17:58.350 --> 00:17:59.970
again, as many of you will know,

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:03.510
how you express things is
of incredible significance

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:05.880
in terms of how you should
understand the reality

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:09.843
that you live in terms of consent.

00:18:11.010 --> 00:18:14.080
So the question I asked
towards the end of that chapter

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:20.130
is how it was possible
for a much weaker state

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:24.120
as Joseon definitely was
to keep its independence?

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:28.860
And I deliberately used the
modern term independence.

00:18:28.860 --> 00:18:32.580
When confronted with
an empire with empires,

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:37.580
that were expansionist and
inclusionists in many ways.

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:42.657
And my answer to that
question is manifold, right?

00:18:42.657 --> 00:18:46.080
One, which I think is truly important,

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:49.920
is through the principle
of always knowing more

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:52.370
about the empire than the
empire knows about you.

00:18:53.765 --> 00:18:55.680
That could be a fancy
name for intelligence,

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:57.390
and it is to some extent,

00:18:57.390 --> 00:18:59.340
though it was much
wider than intelligence.

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:04.340
So the tribute missions
that took place to China,

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:09.480
to the Ming, then to the Ching afterwards,

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:12.840
were also fantastic opportunities
for gathering information

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:15.633
about the empire and
the empire's intentions.

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:20.340
And I would go as far as saying
that if it weren't for that,

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:23.550
then Joseon would have
been much more exposed

00:19:23.550 --> 00:19:27.900
with regard to China than
what was the case in reality,

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:32.900
particularly during the
transition from Ming to Ching,

00:19:33.030 --> 00:19:36.063
which, and I don't have
time to go into that today,

00:19:36.063 --> 00:19:37.827
but also of course, highly problematic

00:19:37.827 --> 00:19:40.320
from a Korean point of view.

00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:41.700
So knowing more about the empire

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:43.380
than the empire knows about you

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:45.003
is a very important part of it.

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:51.740
The other part is of course
to show as Joseon scholars

00:19:53.153 --> 00:19:56.640
and the Joseon state showed increasingly

00:19:56.640 --> 00:20:00.903
as you get into the 17th and 18th century,

00:20:01.980 --> 00:20:06.960
that you can be better
practitioners of Confucianism,

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:09.723
Neo-Confucianism, than
what even the empire can.

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:13.920
This is a remarkable time
period when we think about it

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:15.120
in ideological terms,

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:18.033
in the relationship between
the Ching and the Joseon.

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:23.190
How was it possible for the Joseon kings

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:27.450
to get away after the big changeover,

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:31.530
and after have it confronted and lost

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:35.760
against the Ching armies
in the early 17th century.

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:38.130
Not just to keep its own independence,

00:20:38.130 --> 00:20:41.760
but keep on venerating
long dead Ming emperors

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:46.500
who are of course the sworn
enemies of the Ching Dynasty

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:49.653
that was knowing case in
China, and get away with it.

00:20:52.031 --> 00:20:55.110
And the insistence that
what Confucian scholars

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:59.490
and the Confucian court,
Joseon did in this context,

00:20:59.490 --> 00:21:03.150
was to practice righteousness with regard

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:06.870
to the emperors that had gone before,

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:10.830
and that had had a relationship to Korea.

00:21:10.830 --> 00:21:13.050
I call it in the book
of Azrael relationship.

00:21:13.050 --> 00:21:15.150
It's hard to find an English term

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:16.600
that actually works for this.

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:20.460
Because the important
thing for me is that Korea

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:23.340
is linked to the empire,
but outside of it.

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:25.680
So whatever way you wanna express.

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:27.090
They were allowed to go on doing that,

00:21:27.090 --> 00:21:30.330
because of that shared inheritance

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:32.943
that even the Ching respected.

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:39.140
And that level of even
rather contrary Sinocentrism

00:21:40.764 --> 00:21:43.170
is of course then what gets
into trouble increasingly

00:21:43.170 --> 00:21:45.553
towards the end of the 19th century

00:21:45.553 --> 00:21:49.410
when the Ching itself is receded.

00:21:49.410 --> 00:21:51.690
It's not that the Joseon elite

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:54.720
are particularly fond of
the Ching, they are not.

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:58.110
But living in a world
where the Chinese empire,

00:21:58.110 --> 00:22:01.230
a Chinese empire that Korea
has so long been linked

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:05.970
to in one form or another,
offers both enormous challenges,

00:22:05.970 --> 00:22:09.210
maybe first and foremost in
the relationship to Japan,

00:22:09.210 --> 00:22:12.570
but also enormous
opportunities for rethinking

00:22:12.570 --> 00:22:15.850
what kind of state formation

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:21.570
that a truly independent
career should have.

00:22:21.570 --> 00:22:25.740
Now, that's what then defines

00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:30.510
the second chapter of the book.

00:22:30.510 --> 00:22:34.020
Because it is in many ways an
enforced internationalization

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:37.470
on East Asian that takes
place during this period.

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:40.470
Enforced first and foremost,
because of Japanese aggression,

00:22:41.606 --> 00:22:46.380
against Korean for that
matter, against China as well.

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:48.960
But also because other
countries get into the mix

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:51.660
in terms of defining the framework.

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:55.610
It's so far removed from what you find

00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:57.690
in the early 19th century.

00:22:57.690 --> 00:23:01.323
Never mind the 17th and the 18th century.

00:23:03.018 --> 00:23:08.018
My verdict in the book is that
in spite of being occupied,

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:12.700
of being colonized, losing
its independence to Japan,

00:23:14.370 --> 00:23:16.830
Korea, and being, you know,

00:23:16.830 --> 00:23:19.020
suffering the consequences of that,

00:23:19.020 --> 00:23:21.750
almost immediately in Korea.

00:23:21.750 --> 00:23:24.720
You have this transition over to new forms

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:27.130
of thinking about nation and nationalism

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:30.793
coming out of the late 19th century.

00:23:30.793 --> 00:23:33.383
One could almost go as far as saying that

00:23:33.383 --> 00:23:37.200
part of the tragedy of
Korea in the 20th century

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:40.484
was that just as these new concept

00:23:40.484 --> 00:23:44.850
of what Korea consisted came of age,

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:47.320
the country was occupied by Japan.

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:52.320
So these forms of nationalism
all had to grow up in exile.

00:23:54.941 --> 00:23:56.852
And we know what happens to nationalisms

00:23:56.852 --> 00:23:57.960
that grow up in exile.

00:23:57.960 --> 00:23:59.970
They get hypercharged,

00:23:59.970 --> 00:24:02.490
and they get absolutely convinced

00:24:02.490 --> 00:24:05.280
that the only version of
a future for a country

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:07.680
is the version that their particular brand

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:11.193
of concept of nation
or nationalism consist.

00:24:13.260 --> 00:24:16.230
So by saying that, I'm
not saying the same thing

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:18.303
as some historians have been saying,

00:24:19.590 --> 00:24:21.600
including some American historians.

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:24.990
That the division, the origins
of the division of Korea

00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:29.010
should be sought in
those early divergences

00:24:29.010 --> 00:24:30.330
of Korean nationalism.

00:24:30.330 --> 00:24:33.690
I don't think that is enough of itself

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:34.960
as a cause and effect.

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:36.180
I mean, the division of Korea

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:38.730
came out of the Second
World War, and the Cold War,

00:24:38.730 --> 00:24:41.310
and the division of the
two occupation cells,

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:42.780
without any doubt.

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:47.780
But what it did was that it
fueled the concepts of nation

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:51.030
and national aims that then took root

00:24:51.030 --> 00:24:52.920
both in the south and in the north.

00:24:52.920 --> 00:24:55.470
It shaped and formed them in ways

00:24:55.470 --> 00:24:57.810
that are really, really significant.

00:24:57.810 --> 00:25:01.200
So that is then leads up then
to the third and final part

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:05.160
of the book, where I
talk about the relations

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:07.860
between China and the
two Korean states today.

00:25:07.860 --> 00:25:10.140
I'm not gonna go into
that too deeply here,

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:12.543
you can read the book, now also in Korean.

00:25:14.280 --> 00:25:16.980
But I'm basically saying
that much of the current

00:25:16.980 --> 00:25:20.400
Chinese policy towards
the two Korean states

00:25:20.400 --> 00:25:23.610
is based on some of those
principles and ideas

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:27.677
that come out of the past,
but in a new kind of format.

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:30.450
And that new kind of format

00:25:30.450 --> 00:25:34.290
is definitely based on China's own rights,

00:25:34.290 --> 00:25:37.260
the PLC's own rights, as an economic power

00:25:37.260 --> 00:25:38.703
over the past generation.

00:25:40.530 --> 00:25:44.700
Some ideas in that right
have come from Korea,

00:25:44.700 --> 00:25:48.120
just as some ideas about the organization

00:25:48.120 --> 00:25:51.050
of Confucian society in the Ming

00:25:51.050 --> 00:25:53.853
in the 16th century came from Korea.

00:25:54.840 --> 00:25:59.580
But there are also an increasing,
and to me worrisome level,

00:25:59.580 --> 00:26:04.050
within the PLC of developing a form

00:26:04.050 --> 00:26:08.430
of Chinese nationalism
that projects two Korea,

00:26:08.430 --> 00:26:11.463
to both Koreas and to other
countries in the region,

00:26:12.300 --> 00:26:16.290
a new form of Sinocentrism
that go beyond what you saw

00:26:16.290 --> 00:26:21.243
even during the Ming and
probably also the Ching empire.

00:26:24.090 --> 00:26:27.700
It is a form of putting
China at the center

00:26:28.980 --> 00:26:31.860
that asks not for linkages,

00:26:31.860 --> 00:26:35.283
and that form of intellectual
cultural inclusion.

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:39.380
Even under pressure has happened
in the case of the Ching.

00:26:40.737 --> 00:26:43.113
But in diplomatic and strategic terms,

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:48.377
and will punish countries at
the borders that do not profess

00:26:50.370 --> 00:26:52.980
to the same understanding
of that centrality,

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:54.430
that central role of China

00:26:55.530 --> 00:27:00.530
as the current PLC leaders professed.

00:27:00.600 --> 00:27:01.433
That worries me.

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:03.570
It doesn't.

00:27:03.570 --> 00:27:05.250
Not because of the way some of

00:27:05.250 --> 00:27:06.847
our American colleagues are saying,

00:27:06.847 --> 00:27:09.240
"Oh, this is bringing China
back to the way it was

00:27:09.240 --> 00:27:10.320
during the great dynasties."

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:11.343
It's not doing that.

00:27:12.300 --> 00:27:15.930
It is presenting us with a
completely new kind of problem

00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:20.930
in terms of how China's fantastic
race, an admirable race,

00:27:21.270 --> 00:27:24.750
will influence the smaller
countries that surround it,

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:27.123
whether that will be a cooperative form,

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:33.440
or authoritarian, and
forced form of cooperation

00:27:34.080 --> 00:27:35.880
between China and these countries.

00:27:35.880 --> 00:27:39.450
So there, that's where I
leave it in terms of the book.

00:27:39.450 --> 00:27:42.960
It's a very brief book, so
you might be able to read it

00:27:42.960 --> 00:27:46.170
in a couple of afternoons if
you have an interest in it.

00:27:46.170 --> 00:27:48.300
I don't write many brief books.

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:50.820
So if you want to read
a brief book of mine,

00:27:50.820 --> 00:27:53.280
this might be a good place to stop.

00:27:53.280 --> 00:27:54.690
So that is what I had to say.

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:55.555
Thank you.

00:27:55.555 --> 00:27:58.722
(audience applauding)

00:28:01.083 --> 00:28:02.940
- [Hwansoo] Okay now, Professor Youn.

00:28:02.940 --> 00:28:03.773
- Yes, sir.

00:28:08.042 --> 00:28:09.375
Okay, thank you.

00:28:20.839 --> 00:28:21.672
Hi, everyone.

00:28:21.672 --> 00:28:23.280
My name is Myung Chul Youn.

00:28:23.280 --> 00:28:24.113
Yes, okay.

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:28.243
I'm a professor and a poet.

00:28:28.243 --> 00:28:32.013
And sometimes I say too, I'm an explorer.

00:28:32.850 --> 00:28:34.793
Sometimes, sometimes, okay.

00:28:37.038 --> 00:28:40.955
(speaking in Korean)

00:28:42.870 --> 00:28:47.070
Goguryeo Kingdom is very
important in Korean histories,

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:50.460
and everything, and
especially maritime history

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:52.020
of East Asian.

00:28:52.020 --> 00:28:55.023
This is my main study.

00:28:58.456 --> 00:28:59.289
(speaking in Korean)

00:28:59.289 --> 00:29:00.660
(Juhn speaking in Korean)

00:29:00.660 --> 00:29:02.451
- Okay, I'm sorry. (laughs)

00:29:02.451 --> 00:29:03.534
(Juhn speaking in Korean)

00:29:03.534 --> 00:29:04.423
(speaking in Korean)

00:29:04.423 --> 00:29:08.370
(Juhn speaking in Korean)

00:29:08.370 --> 00:29:10.233
- The books are written me.

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:13.680
Today is very important to me,

00:29:13.680 --> 00:29:17.280
to Koreans, to Americans, and the world.

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:19.893
This is a reason, it is my idea.

00:29:22.794 --> 00:29:26.711
(speaking in Korean)

00:29:30.840 --> 00:29:32.190
- [Juhn] Right now, a lot
of people are interested

00:29:32.190 --> 00:29:35.562
in East Asia, especially
Korea's place within East Asia.

00:29:35.562 --> 00:29:39.990
(speaking in Korean)

00:29:39.990 --> 00:29:41.820
- [Juhn] But a lot of
people have misperceptions

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:44.305
and misconceptions about
East Asia and Korea.

00:29:44.305 --> 00:29:48.780
(speaking in Korean)

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:52.200
- [Juhn] First, there's this
idea that Korea is an appendage

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:57.200
or connected branch of
Chinese history in thought.

00:29:57.213 --> 00:30:01.230
(speaking in Korean)

00:30:01.230 --> 00:30:03.780
- [Juhn] There's also this
idea that Korea's history

00:30:03.780 --> 00:30:05.430
and culture is limited to what happened

00:30:05.430 --> 00:30:07.170
in the Korean Peninsula.

00:30:07.170 --> 00:30:08.003
- Yes, okay.

00:30:08.003 --> 00:30:11.352
Yes, the hermit kingdom and
the land of the morning calm.

00:30:11.352 --> 00:30:15.269
(speaking in Korean)

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:16.973
- [Juhn] Those expressions,

00:30:16.973 --> 00:30:18.840
hermit kingdom and the
land of the morning calm

00:30:18.840 --> 00:30:21.090
are expressions that appeared
in the late 19th century

00:30:21.090 --> 00:30:24.145
and were often used by
foreigners to refer to Korea.

00:30:24.145 --> 00:30:28.062
(speaking in Korean)

00:30:29.520 --> 00:30:31.830
- [Juhn] And that's stuck,
and that's how people continue

00:30:31.830 --> 00:30:33.403
to refer to Korea.

00:30:33.403 --> 00:30:37.320
(speaking in Korean)

00:30:39.450 --> 00:30:40.980
- [Juhn] And there is the misperception

00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:44.250
that Korea has continued to
be a nation that was oppressed

00:30:44.250 --> 00:30:46.660
by either China or Japan.

00:30:46.660 --> 00:30:50.430
(speaking in Korean)

00:30:50.430 --> 00:30:55.380
- [Juhn] And these two
kingdoms in the past,

00:30:55.380 --> 00:30:57.780
Goguryeo and Balhae, are often understood

00:30:57.780 --> 00:31:00.479
just simply as parts of Chinese history.

00:31:00.479 --> 00:31:05.100
(speaking in Korean)

00:31:05.100 --> 00:31:07.530
- [Juhn] And these claims
have been advanced by scholars

00:31:07.530 --> 00:31:09.249
in China recently.

00:31:09.249 --> 00:31:13.200
(speaking in Korean)

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:15.900
- [Juhn] And there is the
claim that has been advanced

00:31:15.900 --> 00:31:20.900
that ancient Korea was dominated by Japan.

00:31:21.032 --> 00:31:24.949
(speaking in Korean)

00:31:30.240 --> 00:31:32.040
- [Juhn] I'm a historian,
I work with facts,

00:31:32.040 --> 00:31:35.316
and I believe that these
claims are not based on facts.

00:31:35.316 --> 00:31:39.233
(speaking in Korean)

00:31:40.980 --> 00:31:42.780
- [Juhn] We need to
look at Korean history.

00:31:42.780 --> 00:31:43.890
So let's start with this map.

00:31:43.890 --> 00:31:45.922
This is a map of Eurasia.

00:31:45.922 --> 00:31:49.839
(speaking in Korean)

00:31:51.411 --> 00:31:53.370
- [Juhn] In my view,
there are six major paths

00:31:53.370 --> 00:31:56.538
of connections that connected Eurasia.

00:31:56.538 --> 00:32:00.455
(speaking in Korean)

00:32:05.730 --> 00:32:06.930
- [Juhn] The most important are the three

00:32:06.930 --> 00:32:09.780
that he just pointed out, the
steppe road, the silk road,

00:32:09.780 --> 00:32:12.571
and the maritime trade route
road on the bottom left.

00:32:12.571 --> 00:32:15.210
(speaking in Korean)

00:32:15.210 --> 00:32:17.160
- [Juhn] And it's very
important to understand

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:20.793
these geographic developments,
to understand Korea properly.

00:32:20.793 --> 00:32:24.930
(speaking in Korean)

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:27.660
- [Juhn] Koreans use
the word the Han people.

00:32:27.660 --> 00:32:29.476
- Han people for the nation.

00:32:29.476 --> 00:32:33.393
(speaking in Korean)

00:32:34.652 --> 00:32:36.810
- [Juhn] And this is very
different from the way the word

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:38.795
nation is used by other countries.

00:32:38.795 --> 00:32:41.730
(speaking in Korean)

00:32:41.730 --> 00:32:44.633
- [Juhn] I'll explain
what that means next time.

00:32:44.633 --> 00:32:45.840
- Friday.
- Friday.

00:32:45.840 --> 00:32:46.673
- Okay, Friday.

00:32:46.673 --> 00:32:48.447
Yes, Friday. (laughs)

00:32:48.447 --> 00:32:52.364
(speaking in Korean)

00:32:54.120 --> 00:32:56.190
- [Juhn] So the emergence,
as it says there,

00:32:56.190 --> 00:32:57.930
the emergence of the nation's ancestor,

00:32:57.930 --> 00:33:00.870
or the origins of the Korean ethnic group,

00:33:00.870 --> 00:33:05.676
dates back to 15 to 12 century BCE.

00:33:05.676 --> 00:33:09.750
(speaking in Korean)

00:33:09.750 --> 00:33:13.530
- [Juhn] There wasn't a
complete radical displacement

00:33:13.530 --> 00:33:15.485
of the people in the Korean Peninsula.

00:33:15.485 --> 00:33:19.402
(speaking in Korean)

00:33:23.370 --> 00:33:26.010
- [Juhn] So a lot of
cultures and people came in,

00:33:26.010 --> 00:33:28.410
but it blended with the existing

00:33:28.410 --> 00:33:30.330
local cultures and traditions.

00:33:30.330 --> 00:33:33.091
because the local cultures
and traditions were so strong.

00:33:33.091 --> 00:33:37.008
(speaking in Korean)

00:33:39.780 --> 00:33:42.739
- [Juhn] There were two occasions
when Korea was conquered.

00:33:42.739 --> 00:33:46.656
(speaking in Korean)

00:33:49.952 --> 00:33:51.120
- [Juhn] But that didn't result

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:54.278
in the complete transformation
of the Korean people.

00:33:54.278 --> 00:33:58.195
(speaking in Korean)

00:34:00.480 --> 00:34:02.850
- [Juhn] And this is a
unique characteristic

00:34:02.850 --> 00:34:05.430
that makes it very different
from the Mongols, Turks,

00:34:05.430 --> 00:34:06.741
and others.
- Okay.

00:34:06.741 --> 00:34:10.658
(speaking in Korean)

00:34:13.680 --> 00:34:16.950
- [Juhn] So he, Professor
Youn, calls Korea a living

00:34:16.950 --> 00:34:19.833
in a cultural and also
a historical community.

00:34:19.833 --> 00:34:23.750
(speaking in Korean)

00:34:24.810 --> 00:34:26.400
- [Juhn] And because these are so strong,

00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:29.050
Koreans have this sense, a
strong sense of community.

00:34:31.737 --> 00:34:36.030
(speaking in Korean)

00:34:36.030 --> 00:34:37.710
- [Juhn] A lot of people
are interested in the issue

00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:40.253
of how Koreans emerge,
or where Koreans emerge.

00:34:40.253 --> 00:34:44.170
(speaking in Korean)

00:34:46.440 --> 00:34:49.740
- [Juhn] Professor Youn's theory
is that eight route for use

00:34:49.740 --> 00:34:52.074
for people to migrate
into the Korean Peninsula.

00:34:52.074 --> 00:34:55.991
(speaking in Korean)

00:34:59.880 --> 00:35:01.830
- [Juhn] So the three routes
that were mentioned earlier

00:35:01.830 --> 00:35:04.890
through the Baikal area, and
also through the Silk Road,

00:35:04.890 --> 00:35:07.320
and the maritime route were
the three most important routes

00:35:07.320 --> 00:35:09.333
to which people entered the peninsula.

00:35:10.345 --> 00:35:14.262
(speaking in Korean)

00:35:24.150 --> 00:35:27.360
- [Juhn] So early Koreans
were influenced very heavily

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:28.350
by northern peoples,

00:35:28.350 --> 00:35:31.881
especially those who
speak the Altaic language.

00:35:31.881 --> 00:35:35.798
(speaking in Korean)

00:35:36.660 --> 00:35:39.990
- [Juhn] But there was also a
culture that was transmitted

00:35:39.990 --> 00:35:42.599
via India and Southeast Asia into Korea.

00:35:42.599 --> 00:35:46.516
(speaking in Korean)

00:35:53.460 --> 00:35:55.230
- [Juhn] So in Professor Youn's view,

00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:58.080
the best way to understand
the emergence of Korea

00:35:58.080 --> 00:36:00.405
is to understand as the foundation,

00:36:00.405 --> 00:36:03.570
the entrance of people
through the northern route,

00:36:03.570 --> 00:36:07.170
but also supplemented and grown
through the entry of people

00:36:07.170 --> 00:36:09.320
through the maritime southern route.

00:36:09.320 --> 00:36:13.237
(speaking in Korean)

00:36:16.560 --> 00:36:19.140
- [Juhn] The theory that
the native people of Korea

00:36:19.140 --> 00:36:21.180
originated from the Korean Peninsula,

00:36:21.180 --> 00:36:24.867
this is a theory that is
popular in North Korea.

00:36:24.867 --> 00:36:27.120
(speaking in Korean)

00:36:27.120 --> 00:36:29.152
- [Juhn] I'll provide
an alternative theory.

00:36:29.152 --> 00:36:32.340
(speaking in Korean)

00:36:32.340 --> 00:36:34.710
- [Juhn] I divide East Asian civilizations

00:36:34.710 --> 00:36:37.740
into these three separate civilizations.

00:36:37.740 --> 00:36:39.900
- Chinese civilization, one.

00:36:39.900 --> 00:36:41.640
And the second, northern civilization,

00:36:41.640 --> 00:36:44.302
and the eastern civilization.

00:36:44.302 --> 00:36:46.521
(speaking in Korean)

00:36:46.521 --> 00:36:49.410
- [Juhn] Divide into three
civilization, (laughs)

00:36:49.410 --> 00:36:50.960
Chinese, northern, and eastern.

00:36:54.078 --> 00:36:57.995
(speaking in Korean)

00:37:04.590 --> 00:37:07.420
- [Juhn] So in the eastern
civilization he includes

00:37:09.360 --> 00:37:11.370
the Japanese archipelago, Korea,

00:37:11.370 --> 00:37:13.670
and of course, the lands
of Manchuria as well.

00:37:14.941 --> 00:37:18.858
(speaking in Korean)

00:37:20.790 --> 00:37:21.960
- [Juhn] In a more narrow sense,

00:37:21.960 --> 00:37:24.824
we can think about East Asia this way.

00:37:24.824 --> 00:37:28.741
(speaking in Korean)

00:37:33.211 --> 00:37:35.191
- Professor Youn to understand East Asia

00:37:35.191 --> 00:37:36.462
created a new model,

00:37:36.462 --> 00:37:39.014
and called it the East
Asian Mediterranean Sea.

00:37:39.014 --> 00:37:43.390
(speaking in Korean)

00:37:43.390 --> 00:37:46.200
- And since 20 years ago,
since he developed it,

00:37:46.200 --> 00:37:47.880
this theory has been in wide use

00:37:47.880 --> 00:37:49.696
in other parts of the world as well.

00:37:49.696 --> 00:37:53.613
(speaking in Korean)

00:37:57.600 --> 00:37:59.460
- The Mediterranean Sea,
as you can see here,

00:37:59.460 --> 00:38:01.170
connects various points on this map,

00:38:01.170 --> 00:38:04.770
and connects various
regions and countries today

00:38:04.770 --> 00:38:05.850
into one region.

00:38:05.850 --> 00:38:09.767
(speaking in Korean)

00:38:32.040 --> 00:38:34.260
- [Juhn] So his theory is
different from previous theories.

00:38:34.260 --> 00:38:38.190
In that, he places Korea at
the core of the geopolitics

00:38:38.190 --> 00:38:40.890
of the East Asian Mediterranean Sea.

00:38:40.890 --> 00:38:43.414
He puts Korea as the
hub of the geoeconomics

00:38:43.414 --> 00:38:45.570
of the Mediterranean Sea.

00:38:45.570 --> 00:38:48.150
He also puts Korea in
the interchange point

00:38:48.150 --> 00:38:51.900
of the geo culture of the
East Asia Mediterranean Sea.

00:38:51.900 --> 00:38:53.220
And last but not least,

00:38:53.220 --> 00:38:55.950
an expression that he coined himself,

00:38:55.950 --> 00:38:58.740
he puts Korea at the
heart of the geo mentalogy

00:38:58.740 --> 00:39:02.056
of the East Asian Mediterranean Sea.

00:39:02.056 --> 00:39:04.320
(speaking in Korean)

00:39:04.320 --> 00:39:08.780
- [Juhn] But this does not
apply to the Joseon period.

00:39:08.780 --> 00:39:11.580
(speaking in Korean)

00:39:11.580 --> 00:39:13.560
- [Juhn] This is a map that was created

00:39:13.560 --> 00:39:15.209
in the Joseon period.

00:39:15.209 --> 00:39:19.126
(speaking in Korean)

00:39:30.720 --> 00:39:34.590
- [Juhn] So this, what you see
here is a conception of Korea

00:39:34.590 --> 00:39:37.230
that is no more than a thousand years old.

00:39:37.230 --> 00:39:39.810
And prior to that, the Korean
conception of territory

00:39:39.810 --> 00:39:42.540
was much larger, and it
encompassed everything

00:39:42.540 --> 00:39:46.020
from areas up in Manchuria
down to the Japanese islands.

00:39:46.020 --> 00:39:46.974
- Yeah.

00:39:46.974 --> 00:39:50.891
(speaking in Korean)

00:39:52.920 --> 00:39:54.750
- [Juhn] He's just gonna skip the period,

00:39:54.750 --> 00:39:56.360
because it's well known.

00:39:56.360 --> 00:40:00.277
(speaking in Korean)

00:40:04.951 --> 00:40:08.784
- [Juhn] So Gojoseon is
the country or kingdom

00:40:10.830 --> 00:40:13.440
that Koreans considered to
be the very first kingdom,

00:40:13.440 --> 00:40:16.080
Korean kingdom to have ever existed.

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:17.970
But Professor Youn
actually profess to call it

00:40:17.970 --> 00:40:20.040
not all Joseon, which is
the literal translation

00:40:20.040 --> 00:40:22.879
of the word Gojoseon,
but rather Proto-Joseon.

00:40:22.879 --> 00:40:23.712
- Okay.

00:40:23.712 --> 00:40:25.650
(speaking in Korean)

00:40:25.650 --> 00:40:28.190
- [Juhn] This is my book on Proto-Joseon.

00:40:28.190 --> 00:40:29.818
- Okay.

00:40:29.818 --> 00:40:33.735
(speaking in Korean)

00:40:39.586 --> 00:40:43.770
- [Juhn] Gojoseon's
territory roughly coincides

00:40:43.770 --> 00:40:46.470
with what is today Liaodong
Peninsula, the Middle Manchuria,

00:40:46.470 --> 00:40:48.897
and also middle to north
regions of the Korean Peninsula.

00:40:48.897 --> 00:40:52.814
(speaking in Korean)

00:40:58.260 --> 00:40:59.970
- [Juhn] I use these
four things as evidence

00:40:59.970 --> 00:41:03.818
to claim that that was the
territories of Proto-Joseon.

00:41:03.818 --> 00:41:07.735
(speaking in Korean)

00:41:09.870 --> 00:41:11.190
- [Juhn] There are lot of dolmens,

00:41:11.190 --> 00:41:12.600
these are huge stone structures

00:41:12.600 --> 00:41:13.890
that were constructed everywhere.

00:41:13.890 --> 00:41:16.500
Apparently, there's over 40,000 of these.

00:41:16.500 --> 00:41:18.000
I didn't know that.

00:41:18.000 --> 00:41:19.428
Currently in existence.

00:41:19.428 --> 00:41:23.345
(speaking in Korean)

00:41:30.570 --> 00:41:33.130
- [Juhn] This is a Gojoseon
or Proto-Joseon dolmen

00:41:34.050 --> 00:41:37.251
that currently exists in
Southern Liaodong Peninsula.

00:41:37.251 --> 00:41:39.600
(speaking in Korean)

00:41:39.600 --> 00:41:40.532
- [Juhn] That's him. (laughs)

00:41:40.532 --> 00:41:41.850
(audience laughing)
(speaking in Korean)

00:41:41.850 --> 00:41:43.031
- [Juhn] So you can see how big it is.

00:41:43.031 --> 00:41:44.799
- Okay.

00:41:44.799 --> 00:41:48.716
(speaking in Korean)

00:41:50.430 --> 00:41:52.997
- [Juhn] This is a dolmen
Pyongyang in North Korea today.

00:41:52.997 --> 00:41:54.450
(speaking in Korean)

00:41:54.450 --> 00:41:56.024
- [Juhn] There are a lot of these.

00:41:56.024 --> 00:41:58.590
(speaking in Korean)

00:41:58.590 --> 00:42:00.780
- [Juhn] This is in North Jeolla province

00:42:00.780 --> 00:42:01.710
in South Korea today.

00:42:01.710 --> 00:42:02.859
- Yes.

00:42:02.859 --> 00:42:04.660
(speaking in Korean)

00:42:04.660 --> 00:42:07.255
- [Juhn] This is in South
Jeolla province in South Korea.

00:42:07.255 --> 00:42:11.340
(speaking in Korean)

00:42:11.340 --> 00:42:13.140
- [Juhn] In just that Jeolla region alone,

00:42:13.140 --> 00:42:16.188
there are over 20,000 dolmens.

00:42:16.188 --> 00:42:18.000
(speaking in Korean)

00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:20.190
- [Juhn] This is in North
Gyeongsang province,

00:42:20.190 --> 00:42:22.699
which is in the southeastern
corner of the Korean Peninsula.

00:42:22.699 --> 00:42:26.616
(speaking in Korean)

00:42:28.873 --> 00:42:32.940
- [Juhn] But the shape of the
dolmens in the north and south

00:42:32.940 --> 00:42:35.206
of the Korean Peninsula are different.

00:42:35.206 --> 00:42:38.070
(speaking in Korean)

00:42:38.070 --> 00:42:39.510
- [Juhn] It's not a
fundamental difference,

00:42:39.510 --> 00:42:42.210
but rather a difference in style.

00:42:42.210 --> 00:42:44.790
(speaking in Korean)

00:42:44.790 --> 00:42:46.890
- [Juhn] This is a typical sword

00:42:46.890 --> 00:42:48.953
from the Proto-Joseon period.

00:42:48.953 --> 00:42:51.750
(speaking in Korean)

00:42:51.750 --> 00:42:53.324
- [Juhn] Pretty, isn't it? (laughs)

00:42:53.324 --> 00:42:54.262
- Yes, sir.

00:42:54.262 --> 00:42:58.179
(speaking in Korean)

00:43:01.200 --> 00:43:03.219
One, two, three.

00:43:03.219 --> 00:43:04.680
(speaking in Korean)

00:43:04.680 --> 00:43:07.140
- [Juhn] So he finds a logic in this,

00:43:07.140 --> 00:43:10.889
and he believes that it's can
be divided into three parts.

00:43:10.889 --> 00:43:13.860
(speaking in Korean)

00:43:13.860 --> 00:43:15.660
- [Juhn] He believes
that this wasn't actually

00:43:15.660 --> 00:43:16.800
meant to be used as a weapon,

00:43:16.800 --> 00:43:18.603
but rather as a ritual to implement.

00:43:19.693 --> 00:43:23.682
(speaking in Korean)

00:43:23.682 --> 00:43:24.515
- [Juhn] All right.

00:43:24.515 --> 00:43:27.270
(speaking in Korean)

00:43:27.270 --> 00:43:29.400
- [Juhn] So the image on the left

00:43:29.400 --> 00:43:31.230
is from Liaodong Peninsula,

00:43:31.230 --> 00:43:35.200
and the image on the
right is from the Liaoxi.

00:43:35.200 --> 00:43:38.719
Two opposite directions of the same area.

00:43:38.719 --> 00:43:41.910
(speaking in Korean)

00:43:41.910 --> 00:43:43.860
- [Juhn] And this is an
example of that blade

00:43:43.860 --> 00:43:45.646
that was found in Japan.

00:43:45.646 --> 00:43:49.563
(speaking in Korean)

00:43:51.522 --> 00:43:52.620
(audience laughing)

00:43:52.620 --> 00:43:56.520
- [Juhn] So that's drawing
of the thing that you see

00:43:56.520 --> 00:43:58.308
on the right there, it
was done by his wife.

00:43:58.308 --> 00:44:01.386
(speaking in Korean)

00:44:01.386 --> 00:44:03.180
- [Juhn] The husband
and wife work together

00:44:03.180 --> 00:44:04.953
to reconstruct Korea's past.

00:44:06.342 --> 00:44:08.250
(speaking in Korean)

00:44:08.250 --> 00:44:10.470
- [Juhn] Nationalists. (laughs)

00:44:10.470 --> 00:44:11.865
Patriot, sorry, patriot.
(audience laughing)

00:44:11.865 --> 00:44:14.486
That's a lot of words, sorry. (laughs)

00:44:14.486 --> 00:44:18.403
(speaking in Korean)

00:44:27.900 --> 00:44:29.250
- [Juhn] So these are bronze mirrors,

00:44:29.250 --> 00:44:31.440
and he believes that the
bronze mirrors in China,

00:44:31.440 --> 00:44:33.840
and the ones that you found
in the northern steppes,

00:44:33.840 --> 00:44:36.270
and also the bronze mirrors
found in the Korean Peninsula

00:44:36.270 --> 00:44:38.378
are all different in style.

00:44:38.378 --> 00:44:42.295
(speaking in Korean)

00:44:45.960 --> 00:44:48.600
- [Juhn] So some bronze mirrors
around the third century

00:44:48.600 --> 00:44:51.960
of the common era from
China, can be found in Japan,

00:44:51.960 --> 00:44:53.580
but there are a lot
more that actually came

00:44:53.580 --> 00:44:54.660
from the Korean Peninsula.

00:44:54.660 --> 00:44:57.000
(speaking in Korean)

00:44:57.000 --> 00:44:58.653
- [Juhn] Can you see the words?

00:44:59.600 --> 00:45:03.517
(speaking in Korean)

00:45:07.880 --> 00:45:08.844
(Juhn speaking in Korean language)

00:45:08.844 --> 00:45:11.220
(speaking in Korean)

00:45:11.220 --> 00:45:12.390
- One, two, three, four, five,

00:45:12.390 --> 00:45:13.689
six, seven, 100.
- So there are

00:45:13.689 --> 00:45:17.580
13,000 lines, and then it's some.

00:45:17.580 --> 00:45:18.413
- Yes.

00:45:18.413 --> 00:45:21.633
(speaking in Korean)

00:45:21.633 --> 00:45:24.000
- [Juhn] And this is between the lines

00:45:24.000 --> 00:45:25.852
is what you see up there.

00:45:25.852 --> 00:45:29.070
(speaking in Korean)

00:45:29.070 --> 00:45:30.540
- [Juhn] And he believes
that this is evidence

00:45:30.540 --> 00:45:32.848
of incredible technology.

00:45:32.848 --> 00:45:36.765
(speaking in Korean)

00:45:38.130 --> 00:45:39.750
- [Juhn] The ability or the capacity

00:45:39.750 --> 00:45:42.070
to create bronze mirrors and bronze swords

00:45:43.590 --> 00:45:47.417
means that they were at the
top of the technological game.

00:45:47.417 --> 00:45:51.334
(speaking in Korean)

00:45:53.700 --> 00:45:56.190
- [Juhn] He wants to, once
again, reemphasize the fact that

00:45:56.190 --> 00:45:58.050
bronze mirrors produced in China,

00:45:58.050 --> 00:46:01.097
and the bronze mirrors produced
in Korea are very different.

00:46:01.097 --> 00:46:05.040
(speaking in Korean)

00:46:05.040 --> 00:46:06.720
- [Juhn] Now that you've
heard what I had to say,

00:46:06.720 --> 00:46:07.950
you can see the significance

00:46:07.950 --> 00:46:09.844
of what you see here on this map, right?

00:46:09.844 --> 00:46:13.761
(speaking in Korean)

00:46:19.036 --> 00:46:22.290
- [Juhn] So when we go beyond the narrow

00:46:22.290 --> 00:46:25.170
understanding of nation, and
we understand it culturally,

00:46:25.170 --> 00:46:28.140
you can see that Korea
encompasses a territory

00:46:28.140 --> 00:46:31.449
that is much bigger than
the way it's imagined today.

00:46:31.449 --> 00:46:35.366
(speaking in Korean)

00:46:38.340 --> 00:46:40.110
- [Juhn] So in the interest of time,

00:46:40.110 --> 00:46:42.900
he's gonna skip over the
fact that there are dolmens

00:46:42.900 --> 00:46:44.430
that are found in other areas

00:46:44.430 --> 00:46:48.135
that are currently China today.

00:46:48.135 --> 00:46:52.052
(speaking in Korean)

00:46:54.570 --> 00:46:57.870
- [Juhn] So around the turn
of the millennium in Korea,

00:46:57.870 --> 00:47:00.990
what you see is the
rise of three kingdoms,

00:47:00.990 --> 00:47:02.370
Goguryeo, Baekje, and Silla.

00:47:02.370 --> 00:47:03.203
- Okay.

00:47:03.203 --> 00:47:04.317
(speaking in Korean)

00:47:04.317 --> 00:47:07.080
- [Juhn] The first is
Goguryeo, as you see here.

00:47:08.237 --> 00:47:12.154
(speaking in Korean)

00:47:14.760 --> 00:47:16.770
- [Juhn] The origins of
this particular kingdom

00:47:16.770 --> 00:47:18.960
is believed to be the middle
part of the Yalu river

00:47:18.960 --> 00:47:20.738
and also middle Manchuria.

00:47:20.738 --> 00:47:24.655
(speaking in Korean)

00:47:27.630 --> 00:47:29.490
- [Juhn] So we should
take note of the fact that

00:47:29.490 --> 00:47:32.670
the origins of Proto-Joseon
and also Goguryeo

00:47:32.670 --> 00:47:35.040
lie outside of the Korean Peninsula,

00:47:35.040 --> 00:47:36.420
and what is today Manchuria.
- This is very important.

00:47:36.420 --> 00:47:37.776
Okay.

00:47:37.776 --> 00:47:41.693
(speaking in Korean)

00:47:43.320 --> 00:47:45.960
- [Juhn] The founder of the
kingdom is named Jumong,

00:47:45.960 --> 00:47:48.960
and he considered himself,
or called himself,

00:47:48.960 --> 00:47:50.201
the son of heaven.

00:47:50.201 --> 00:47:54.118
(speaking in Korean)

00:47:58.410 --> 00:48:00.690
- [Juhn] And he believes
that the heaven here

00:48:00.690 --> 00:48:04.140
does not refer to the heaven
used in the old Jo text,

00:48:04.140 --> 00:48:05.580
or the Confucian classics,

00:48:05.580 --> 00:48:07.470
but rather to the heaven
that is worshiped up

00:48:07.470 --> 00:48:09.799
by the northern steppe
people, like the Mongols.

00:48:09.799 --> 00:48:13.716
(speaking in Korean)

00:48:16.320 --> 00:48:19.380
- [Juhn] And so he
believes that the Goguryeo

00:48:19.380 --> 00:48:22.170
inherited much from Proto-Joseon,

00:48:22.170 --> 00:48:24.587
and also from the northern steppe people.

00:48:24.587 --> 00:48:28.504
(speaking in Korean)

00:48:37.200 --> 00:48:38.790
- [Juhn] So this is a
painting of the father

00:48:38.790 --> 00:48:40.743
of the founder of Goguryeo,

00:48:42.180 --> 00:48:44.910
and he's considered to be God in heaven.

00:48:44.910 --> 00:48:46.590
And this is a painting
that's currently housed

00:48:46.590 --> 00:48:49.920
in North Korea today near
what they believe to be,

00:48:49.920 --> 00:48:52.953
or in the shrine where they
believe to be his tomb.

00:48:53.838 --> 00:48:57.755
(speaking in Korean)

00:49:08.160 --> 00:49:09.450
- [Juhn] He used the name of that God

00:49:09.450 --> 00:49:12.210
for the wrath that he took himself

00:49:12.210 --> 00:49:15.810
to go from an island in Korea
all the way over to Japan,

00:49:15.810 --> 00:49:19.530
just to show you that the
7,000 years of maritime trade

00:49:19.530 --> 00:49:23.486
and exchange did indeed
happen, and could happen.

00:49:23.486 --> 00:49:27.403
(speaking in Korean)

00:49:28.710 --> 00:49:32.610
- [Juhn] He's crossed from
Jiujiang in China over to Korea

00:49:32.610 --> 00:49:34.050
three times already.

00:49:34.050 --> 00:49:34.964
- Yeah.

00:49:34.964 --> 00:49:38.881
(speaking in Korean)

00:49:50.776 --> 00:49:53.447
(Juhn speaking in Korean)

00:49:53.447 --> 00:49:56.637
(speaking in Korean)

00:49:56.637 --> 00:49:58.890
- So the travel from China to an island

00:49:58.890 --> 00:50:01.770
called Heuksando was 17 days.

00:50:01.770 --> 00:50:04.380
And he also took a raft to travel

00:50:04.380 --> 00:50:06.780
all the way over to Japan
as well, which took,

00:50:06.780 --> 00:50:10.530
I think he said 43 days out
of which 10 were actually

00:50:10.530 --> 00:50:12.840
he spent in sail.

00:50:12.840 --> 00:50:16.290
(speaking in Korean)

00:50:16.290 --> 00:50:18.602
- I look weak, but trust me, I'm not.

00:50:18.602 --> 00:50:20.944
(audience laughing)

00:50:20.944 --> 00:50:24.861
(speaking in Korean)

00:50:53.190 --> 00:50:56.550
- [Juhn] So he wants to make sure that,

00:50:56.550 --> 00:50:58.620
he makes it very clear that
what you're actually seeing here

00:50:58.620 --> 00:51:00.870
is not necessarily a concept of territory,

00:51:00.870 --> 00:51:02.670
but rather area of influence.

00:51:02.670 --> 00:51:04.440
And this area of influence was real,

00:51:04.440 --> 00:51:06.930
and it reached, as you can
see, far really up north

00:51:06.930 --> 00:51:08.920
into territories that are still

00:51:10.020 --> 00:51:11.490
subjects of controversy today,

00:51:11.490 --> 00:51:13.589
especially among scholars
of China and Japan.

00:51:13.589 --> 00:51:17.506
(speaking in Korean)

00:51:20.100 --> 00:51:22.140
- [Juhn] And he reconstruct
this area of influence

00:51:22.140 --> 00:51:24.630
using archeological and textual evidence.

00:51:24.630 --> 00:51:27.060
So he doesn't think that
there's really any room

00:51:27.060 --> 00:51:30.307
for different interpretations.

00:51:30.307 --> 00:51:34.224
(speaking in Korean)

00:51:43.482 --> 00:51:46.380
- [Juhn] So at the height
of Goguryeo's history,

00:51:46.380 --> 00:51:51.380
China was in disarray
fighting amongst each other

00:51:52.200 --> 00:51:57.200
and kingdoms like Goguryeo,
and other northern tribes

00:51:57.810 --> 00:52:00.000
took advantage of this fact

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:03.456
to increase their influence in that area.

00:52:03.456 --> 00:52:06.330
(speaking in Korean)

00:52:06.330 --> 00:52:08.207
- [Juhn] I'll tell you a fun story.

00:52:08.207 --> 00:52:12.124
(speaking in Korean)

00:52:26.444 --> 00:52:29.660
- [Juhn] So the fun story is
the folks that you see here,

00:52:29.660 --> 00:52:33.060
the Rouran, I think, they
traveled far out west,

00:52:33.060 --> 00:52:34.950
and they reached all the way over to Italy

00:52:34.950 --> 00:52:36.663
where they settled.

00:52:36.663 --> 00:52:40.580
(speaking in Korean)

00:52:47.350 --> 00:52:49.380
- [Juhn] Goguryeo was able to
take advantage of the split

00:52:49.380 --> 00:52:52.860
of China in Northern Wei
and the Southern Song,

00:52:52.860 --> 00:52:55.033
and engaged in long distance trade.

00:52:55.033 --> 00:52:58.710
(speaking in Korean)

00:52:58.710 --> 00:53:01.830
- [Juhn] And the person
who facilitated all this

00:53:01.830 --> 00:53:06.092
is known to be King Gwanggaeto,
who still you see here.

00:53:06.092 --> 00:53:10.009
(speaking in Korean)

00:53:11.700 --> 00:53:15.210
- [Juhn] The stele is 6.39 meters tall,

00:53:15.210 --> 00:53:19.450
and it contains over 1,700 words.

00:53:19.450 --> 00:53:23.367
(speaking in Korean)

00:53:34.270 --> 00:53:35.250
- [Juhn] What he wants to emphasize

00:53:35.250 --> 00:53:38.760
is not necessarily the size
or the ambitions of the king,

00:53:38.760 --> 00:53:40.950
but rather the place of Korea

00:53:40.950 --> 00:53:44.660
within the larger geopolitical
system on that period.

00:53:44.660 --> 00:53:48.577
(speaking in Korean)

00:53:53.760 --> 00:53:55.470
- [Juhn] And what he
wants to emphasize is that

00:53:55.470 --> 00:53:58.440
the place of Goguryeo in this
larger geopolitical system

00:53:58.440 --> 00:53:59.610
was a peaceful one.

00:53:59.610 --> 00:54:00.780
And that's what they aspired,

00:54:00.780 --> 00:54:02.703
and that was a worldview at the time.

00:54:03.747 --> 00:54:07.664
(speaking in Korean)

00:54:08.670 --> 00:54:12.689
- And the beauty, and the
logic, and the meaning.

00:54:12.689 --> 00:54:13.800
(speaking in Korean)

00:54:13.800 --> 00:54:15.780
- [Juhn] So he wrote
something about this stele,

00:54:15.780 --> 00:54:18.300
and the title contains those
words that you just heard,

00:54:18.300 --> 00:54:20.173
beauty, logic, that's all.

00:54:21.517 --> 00:54:25.434
(speaking in Korean)

00:54:27.250 --> 00:54:29.079
- [Juhn] Guys, that's the king's tomb.

00:54:29.079 --> 00:54:31.710
(Youn speaking in Korean)

00:54:31.710 --> 00:54:33.110
- [Juhn] And it's a pyramid.

00:54:34.291 --> 00:54:35.340
(speaking in Korean)

00:54:35.340 --> 00:54:37.167
- [Juhn] And this is what
that pyramid looks like.

00:54:37.167 --> 00:54:41.084
(speaking in Korean)

00:54:44.100 --> 00:54:45.090
- [Juhn] Tombs likes like this,

00:54:45.090 --> 00:54:46.320
they're over 12 in Manchuria,

00:54:46.320 --> 00:54:47.910
but the only one that currently remains

00:54:47.910 --> 00:54:49.437
is this one that you see here.

00:54:49.437 --> 00:54:53.354
(speaking in Korean)

00:54:54.470 --> 00:54:55.303
- [Juhn] On Friday,

00:54:55.303 --> 00:54:58.350
he'll delve deeper into the philosophical

00:54:58.350 --> 00:54:59.951
meanings of that too.

00:54:59.951 --> 00:55:01.080
(speaking in Korean)

00:55:01.080 --> 00:55:02.630
- [Juhn] So please come Friday.

00:55:03.744 --> 00:55:07.661
(speaking in Korean)

00:55:11.130 --> 00:55:14.190
- [Juhn] So in year 427,
Goguryeo moved its capital

00:55:14.190 --> 00:55:18.000
to what is today North
Korea, to its new palace,

00:55:18.000 --> 00:55:19.153
the Anhak Palace.

00:55:19.153 --> 00:55:21.150
(speaking in Korean)

00:55:21.150 --> 00:55:23.450
- [Juhn] And that's the
castle from that area.

00:55:24.371 --> 00:55:28.288
(speaking in Korean)

00:55:31.080 --> 00:55:31.920
- [Juhn] This is from a mural

00:55:31.920 --> 00:55:33.722
from the fifth and sixth century CE.

00:55:33.722 --> 00:55:36.600
(speaking in Korean)

00:55:36.600 --> 00:55:39.570
- [Juhn] And this is a depiction
of a warrior of an army

00:55:39.570 --> 00:55:43.251
that boasted one of the most
powerful armies at the time.

00:55:43.251 --> 00:55:47.168
(speaking in Korean)

00:55:51.720 --> 00:55:53.970
- [Juhn] So this predicts the Turkish

00:55:53.970 --> 00:55:56.884
depictions of the warriors
by about 100 years.

00:55:56.884 --> 00:55:58.950
(speaking in Korean)

00:55:58.950 --> 00:56:00.150
- [Juhn] And on Friday, I'll talk about

00:56:00.150 --> 00:56:01.283
the function of these warriors.

00:56:01.283 --> 00:56:02.400
(speaking in Korean)

00:56:02.400 --> 00:56:04.678
- [Juhn] Let's now talk about
Baekje, the other kingdom.

00:56:04.678 --> 00:56:08.595
(speaking in Korean)

00:56:11.482 --> 00:56:12.315
- [Juhn] Baekje was established

00:56:12.315 --> 00:56:14.193
by people who fled from Goguryeo.

00:56:15.080 --> 00:56:18.997
(speaking in Korean)

00:56:20.160 --> 00:56:21.810
- [Juhn] And at the
beginning for that reason,

00:56:21.810 --> 00:56:25.050
they frequently had
skirmishes with Goguryeo,

00:56:25.050 --> 00:56:26.610
but they later started to have wars

00:56:26.610 --> 00:56:29.160
with their neighbor in the south Silla.

00:56:29.160 --> 00:56:31.920
(speaking in Korean)

00:56:31.920 --> 00:56:33.390
- [Juhn] And around fourth century CE,

00:56:33.390 --> 00:56:35.400
they began to expand
their areas of influence

00:56:35.400 --> 00:56:37.742
to the Japanese archipelago.

00:56:37.742 --> 00:56:41.659
(speaking in Korean)

00:56:42.869 --> 00:56:44.910
- [Juhn] This is Baekje's first capital.

00:56:44.910 --> 00:56:47.970
And the river that you see
there is the famous Han River,

00:56:47.970 --> 00:56:51.163
which splits the capital Seoul.

00:56:51.163 --> 00:56:55.080
(speaking in Korean)

00:56:57.180 --> 00:56:58.470
- [Juhn] You can see the outlines

00:56:58.470 --> 00:57:00.300
of the original wall of the capitol,

00:57:00.300 --> 00:57:02.370
and you can see that it's
located right by the river,

00:57:02.370 --> 00:57:06.199
which means that this
was a maritime kingdom.

00:57:06.199 --> 00:57:10.116
(speaking in Korean)

00:57:11.610 --> 00:57:13.500
- [Juhn] A lot of these
ancient cities from this period

00:57:13.500 --> 00:57:15.660
are maritime cities,
and they were designed

00:57:15.660 --> 00:57:17.236
to take advantage of maritime.

00:57:17.236 --> 00:57:18.210
(speaking in Korean)

00:57:18.210 --> 00:57:19.810
- [Juhn] That's history.

00:57:19.810 --> 00:57:23.727
(speaking in Korean)

00:57:25.770 --> 00:57:29.760
- [Juhn] So this is a castle
that was used by Goguryeo

00:57:29.760 --> 00:57:31.691
as their military for.

00:57:31.691 --> 00:57:35.608
(speaking in Korean)

00:57:39.002 --> 00:57:41.280
- [Juhn] And that's why you
see around the Han River

00:57:41.280 --> 00:57:43.860
Baekje period pyramids that
look a lot like the pyramids

00:57:43.860 --> 00:57:44.840
from Goguryeo.

00:57:44.840 --> 00:57:48.990
(speaking in Korean)

00:57:48.990 --> 00:57:50.550
- [Juhn] So there are
a lot of these pyramids

00:57:50.550 --> 00:57:52.693
from the Goguryeo in Manchuria.

00:57:52.693 --> 00:57:56.430
(speaking in Korean)

00:57:56.430 --> 00:57:57.815
- [Juhn] He went there 70 times.

00:57:57.815 --> 00:58:01.198
- Yes, okay.

00:58:01.198 --> 00:58:05.115
(speaking in Korean)

00:58:08.490 --> 00:58:10.260
- [Juhn] The last kingdom
to emerge is Silla,

00:58:10.260 --> 00:58:14.643
the founder is known to be
a man named Hyeokgeose Khan.

00:58:15.720 --> 00:58:18.035
(speaking in Korean)

00:58:18.035 --> 00:58:18.868
- Okay.

00:58:18.868 --> 00:58:22.785
(speaking in Korean)

00:58:24.210 --> 00:58:26.670
- [Juhn] The word Kan in this context

00:58:26.670 --> 00:58:28.500
refers the ruler or the nation.

00:58:28.500 --> 00:58:30.690
- Genghis Khan, no.

00:58:30.690 --> 00:58:33.000
Genghis Khan is the correct.

00:58:33.000 --> 00:58:34.382
Genghis Khan.

00:58:34.382 --> 00:58:38.299
(speaking in Korean)

00:58:51.867 --> 00:58:54.030
- [Juhn] And that word Khan
comes from Altaic languages,

00:58:54.030 --> 00:58:58.260
and is shared among people who
want to refer to the ruler.

00:58:58.260 --> 00:58:59.415
To refer to the ruler.

00:58:59.415 --> 00:59:03.332
(speaking in Korean)

00:59:10.950 --> 00:59:13.050
- [Juhn] So the map of
Goguryeo is a little off,

00:59:13.050 --> 00:59:14.700
it should be a bit lower at the top there,

00:59:14.700 --> 00:59:16.220
but what you're seeing here is Silla

00:59:16.220 --> 00:59:18.125
at the height of its power.

00:59:18.125 --> 00:59:22.042
(speaking in Korean)

00:59:35.083 --> 00:59:38.640
- [Juhn] So Koreans tend to,
or prefer to see Goguryeo

00:59:38.640 --> 00:59:41.160
as being much larger than it actually was.

00:59:41.160 --> 00:59:44.310
But the territories that
Goguryeo dispatch officials,

00:59:44.310 --> 00:59:48.823
and controlled indirectly
was a much smaller area.

00:59:48.823 --> 00:59:52.740
(speaking in Korean)

00:59:56.970 --> 00:59:58.680
- [Juhn] He highlighted
this fact in red here,

00:59:58.680 --> 01:00:00.780
because he wants to
emphasize the fact that

01:00:00.780 --> 01:00:02.850
around the fifth to the sixth century,

01:00:02.850 --> 01:00:04.800
there seemed to be culturally a connection

01:00:04.800 --> 01:00:07.940
between Silla and the Xiongnu up north.

01:00:07.940 --> 01:00:09.420
(speaking in Korean)

01:00:09.420 --> 01:00:10.980
- [Juhn] This is a very
complicated question.

01:00:10.980 --> 01:00:11.878
- Yes.

01:00:11.878 --> 01:00:15.795
(speaking in Korean)

01:00:18.630 --> 01:00:20.160
- [Juhn] So these are tomb from Silla

01:00:20.160 --> 01:00:23.610
when you excavate them,
what you find are remnants

01:00:23.610 --> 01:00:25.920
of cultural patterns that you can connect

01:00:25.920 --> 01:00:28.467
with the northern people like the Xiongnu.

01:00:28.467 --> 01:00:32.550
(speaking in Korean)

01:00:32.550 --> 01:00:35.632
- [Juhn] That's the Silla
palace, reconstruction of Anapji.

01:00:35.632 --> 01:00:39.549
(speaking in Korean)

01:00:43.560 --> 01:00:45.370
- [Juhn] This is a
golden crown from Silla.

01:00:45.370 --> 01:00:50.370
Silla is hands down, the most
prolific producer of these,

01:00:50.730 --> 01:00:51.908
of golden crowns.

01:00:51.908 --> 01:00:53.010
(speaking in Korean)

01:00:53.010 --> 01:00:53.843
- [Juhn] It's a fact.

01:00:53.843 --> 01:00:55.248
- Yes.

01:00:55.248 --> 01:00:59.165
(speaking in Korean)

01:01:06.780 --> 01:01:08.640
- [Juhn] So that crown that
you see there on the right

01:01:08.640 --> 01:01:10.320
is from Kazakhstan, and
there are a lot of people

01:01:10.320 --> 01:01:13.092
who draw connections.

01:01:13.092 --> 01:01:15.120
(speaking in Korean)

01:01:15.120 --> 01:01:18.612
- [Juhn] And on Friday, he's
gonna show you all the crowns.

01:01:18.612 --> 01:01:22.529
(speaking in Korean)

01:01:32.820 --> 01:01:34.740
- [Juhn] So there's another kingdom,

01:01:34.740 --> 01:01:37.800
or federation known as
Gaya, and he believes that

01:01:37.800 --> 01:01:40.110
these are maritime city states, or polis.

01:01:40.110 --> 01:01:42.840
These are really smallest
city states that came together

01:01:42.840 --> 01:01:44.730
as a federation, and that's history.

01:01:44.730 --> 01:01:45.563
- Oh, sorry.

01:01:47.391 --> 01:01:48.224
(speaking in Korean)

01:01:48.224 --> 01:01:50.280
- [Juhn] And those are the
different colors represent

01:01:50.280 --> 01:01:53.303
the different city states
known as the six Gaya.

01:01:53.303 --> 01:01:57.220
(speaking in Korean)

01:02:03.841 --> 01:02:07.440
- [Juhn] So the Gaya cultural group,

01:02:07.440 --> 01:02:09.060
they actually began at the southern tip

01:02:09.060 --> 01:02:10.230
of the Korean Peninsula,

01:02:10.230 --> 01:02:12.180
but they began to expand their territories

01:02:12.180 --> 01:02:14.640
and areas of influence
into Japanese islands.

01:02:14.640 --> 01:02:18.570
And more Gaya influence began
to grow as a consequence

01:02:18.570 --> 01:02:21.256
deeper in the middle of the peninsula.

01:02:21.256 --> 01:02:25.173
(speaking in Korean)

01:02:37.110 --> 01:02:40.770
- [Juhn] So archeological
evidence points to movement

01:02:40.770 --> 01:02:43.110
of Gaya folk into the Japanese islands

01:02:43.110 --> 01:02:45.150
around the fourth century BCE.

01:02:45.150 --> 01:02:49.170
But textual material from
Japan points to that happening

01:02:49.170 --> 01:02:51.283
sometime around the
turn of the millennium.

01:02:51.283 --> 01:02:55.687
(speaking in Korean)

01:02:55.687 --> 01:02:58.230
- [Juhn] The closest place
to get to Japan from Korea

01:02:58.230 --> 01:02:59.460
is the city of Busan,

01:02:59.460 --> 01:03:02.242
which is where was under
controlled by Gaya.

01:03:02.242 --> 01:03:04.320
(speaking in Korean)

01:03:04.320 --> 01:03:05.700
- [Juhn] And it's an area where you find

01:03:05.700 --> 01:03:08.068
a lot of Gaya period.

01:03:08.068 --> 01:03:11.985
(speaking in Korean)

01:03:13.590 --> 01:03:15.840
- [Juhn] And that's a cultural artifact

01:03:15.840 --> 01:03:17.220
that was unearthed near Busan.

01:03:17.220 --> 01:03:19.360
And you can see that it's the remains of

01:03:20.331 --> 01:03:21.443
(speaking in Korean).

01:03:21.443 --> 01:03:23.040
(speaking in Korean)

01:03:23.040 --> 01:03:24.090
- [Juhn] And obviously,

01:03:24.090 --> 01:03:26.580
it was influenced heavily
by Goguryeo culture

01:03:26.580 --> 01:03:27.807
- Okay.
- up north.

01:03:29.384 --> 01:03:33.301
(speaking in Korean)

01:03:36.480 --> 01:03:38.910
- [Juhn] A lot of people don't
understand Korean history,

01:03:38.910 --> 01:03:40.710
but what they really
don't understand is that

01:03:40.710 --> 01:03:44.100
Korea was heavily
engaged in maritime trade

01:03:44.100 --> 01:03:46.465
and interaction with other areas.

01:03:46.465 --> 01:03:48.630
(speaking in Korean)

01:03:48.630 --> 01:03:52.609
- [Juhn] This is a book
maritime history in Korea.

01:03:52.609 --> 01:03:54.360
(speaking in Korean)

01:03:54.360 --> 01:03:56.245
- [Juhn] It's been
translated to Vietnamese.

01:03:56.245 --> 01:04:00.270
(speaking in Korean)

01:04:00.270 --> 01:04:02.010
- [Juhn] They tried to
translate it to Chinese,

01:04:02.010 --> 01:04:04.265
but they didn't get the
permissions to do so.

01:04:04.265 --> 01:04:05.098
- Yeah.

01:04:05.098 --> 01:04:09.150
(speaking in Korean)

01:04:09.150 --> 01:04:12.516
- [Juhn] So it was published
in Chinese but in Korea.

01:04:12.516 --> 01:04:17.040
(speaking in Korean)

01:04:17.040 --> 01:04:18.150
- [Juhn] He really wants this book

01:04:18.150 --> 01:04:20.430
to be translated into
Japanese and English as well.

01:04:20.430 --> 01:04:21.656
- Yes.

01:04:21.656 --> 01:04:24.210
(speaking in Korean)

01:04:24.210 --> 01:04:27.120
- [Juhn] This is a depiction
of the maritime trade routes

01:04:27.120 --> 01:04:28.819
of Goguryeo.

01:04:28.819 --> 01:04:30.570
(speaking in Korean)

01:04:30.570 --> 01:04:33.434
- [Juhn] And these are the
maritime trade routes of Baekje.

01:04:33.434 --> 01:04:37.351
(speaking in Korean)

01:04:46.320 --> 01:04:47.760
- [Juhn] So the Northern
Wei was established

01:04:47.760 --> 01:04:49.680
by Xianbei people from up north.

01:04:49.680 --> 01:04:52.260
And those who fled the
incursion of the Northern Wei,

01:04:52.260 --> 01:04:55.170
they established the
kingdoms out in the south,

01:04:55.170 --> 01:04:58.432
the Song, the Southern Qi,
the Liang, and the Jin.

01:04:58.432 --> 01:05:01.860
(speaking in Korean)

01:05:01.860 --> 01:05:05.080
- [Juhn] And the Silla folk
also traveled across the street

01:05:06.060 --> 01:05:09.315
to the Japanese islands through
these maritime trade routes.

01:05:09.315 --> 01:05:13.232
(speaking in Korean)

01:05:23.070 --> 01:05:24.630
- [Juhn] We use archeological artifacts,

01:05:24.630 --> 01:05:27.840
and also Japanese records like
the Kojiki and Nihon Shoki

01:05:27.840 --> 01:05:29.940
to reconstruct these trades.
- Okay.

01:05:29.940 --> 01:05:32.910
(speaking in Korean)

01:05:32.910 --> 01:05:37.244
- [Juhn] And that's the
path of influence of Gaya.

01:05:37.244 --> 01:05:41.161
(speaking in Korean)

01:05:45.870 --> 01:05:47.550
- [Juhn] And I explained
all of this in my book

01:05:47.550 --> 01:05:49.365
on ancient Japan.

01:05:49.365 --> 01:05:53.282
(speaking in Korean)

01:05:55.830 --> 01:05:57.090
- [Juhn] This is a very important bed.

01:05:57.090 --> 01:05:59.220
This is an international
war that takes place

01:05:59.220 --> 01:06:01.833
during this period at the
end of the sixth century.

01:06:01.833 --> 01:06:05.750
(speaking in Korean)

01:06:15.270 --> 01:06:18.207
- [Juhn] So the system of the
three countries, China, Korea,

01:06:18.207 --> 01:06:21.990
and Japan emerges in the wake
of this international war.

01:06:21.990 --> 01:06:24.630
And this was really
the consequence of Tang

01:06:24.630 --> 01:06:27.900
emerging victorious in this war.

01:06:27.900 --> 01:06:30.240
(speaking in Korean)

01:06:30.240 --> 01:06:32.795
- [Juhn] And it's a war
that lasted over 70 years.

01:06:32.795 --> 01:06:36.712
(speaking in Korean)

01:06:42.154 --> 01:06:44.938
- [Juhn] And those are the
reasons behind the war.

01:06:44.938 --> 01:06:48.855
(speaking in Korean)

01:06:56.220 --> 01:07:00.270
- [Juhn] So it's a war
between China and Goguryeo,

01:07:00.270 --> 01:07:04.110
but in direct or indirect
ways, everybody else,

01:07:04.110 --> 01:07:06.720
the surrounding states
and policies were involved

01:07:06.720 --> 01:07:08.245
in this international war as well.

01:07:08.245 --> 01:07:09.360
(speaking in Korean)

01:07:09.360 --> 01:07:11.579
- [Juhn] And this is what that
war basically looked like.

01:07:11.579 --> 01:07:15.912
(Youn speaking in Korean)

01:07:19.740 --> 01:07:20.790
- [Juhn] And that's how many soldiers

01:07:20.790 --> 01:07:22.950
were involved in the war between Sui

01:07:22.950 --> 01:07:26.048
that is the predecessor to
the Tang Dynasty and Goguryeo.

01:07:26.048 --> 01:07:29.280
(speaking in Korean)

01:07:29.280 --> 01:07:32.280
- [Juhn] So Sui Dynasty
loses that war with Goguryeo,

01:07:32.280 --> 01:07:34.693
and collapses in 40 years.

01:07:34.693 --> 01:07:38.610
(speaking in Korean)

01:07:45.112 --> 01:07:47.700
- [Juhn] Tang's Taizong invades

01:07:47.700 --> 01:07:51.420
with a troop of 100,000
with 500 ships, and so on,

01:07:51.420 --> 01:07:54.030
but they actually lose that battle.

01:07:54.030 --> 01:07:57.210
(speaking in Korean)

01:07:57.210 --> 01:07:59.657
- [Juhn] And these are
again all based on facts.

01:07:59.657 --> 01:08:03.574
(speaking in Korean)

01:08:07.470 --> 01:08:08.880
- [Juhn] So this is the
third and last word,

01:08:08.880 --> 01:08:10.980
the first again was
between Sui and Goguryeo.

01:08:10.980 --> 01:08:12.357
Second, Tang and Goguryeo.

01:08:12.357 --> 01:08:15.838
And the third is the
alliance that was formed

01:08:15.838 --> 01:08:18.660
between Tang, China, and Silla,

01:08:18.660 --> 01:08:21.743
which eventually results in
the collapse of Goguryeo.

01:08:21.743 --> 01:08:25.660
(speaking in Korean)

01:08:34.500 --> 01:08:36.450
- [Juhn] So Baekje is
gonna collapse as well.

01:08:36.450 --> 01:08:38.970
Those red dots that you saw
on the Japanese islands,

01:08:38.970 --> 01:08:42.120
those were force established
by the Baekje immigrants.

01:08:42.120 --> 01:08:44.970
And you can see from this
how the intimate relationship

01:08:44.970 --> 01:08:47.166
between the Baekje
immigrants and Japanese.

01:08:47.166 --> 01:08:51.083
(speaking in Korean)

01:08:53.010 --> 01:08:55.110
- [Juhn] And the significance
of this event is that

01:08:55.110 --> 01:08:57.930
it's roughly around this
time that we begin to see

01:08:57.930 --> 01:09:00.744
the rise of what we call Japan today.

01:09:00.744 --> 01:09:05.028
(speaking in Korean)

01:09:05.028 --> 01:09:09.007
- [Juhn] And the name Nihon
Koni, I suppose, in Japanese,

01:09:10.110 --> 01:09:11.640
emerges during this period.

01:09:11.640 --> 01:09:13.255
And you can see the significance of these-

01:09:13.255 --> 01:09:16.492
(speaking in Korean)

01:09:16.492 --> 01:09:18.671
- [Juhn] All in the five minutes.

01:09:18.671 --> 01:09:22.588
(speaking in Korean)

01:09:29.160 --> 01:09:31.740
- [Juhn] So Balhae is a
successor state to Goguryeo,

01:09:31.740 --> 01:09:36.149
and this is advanced in old
Chinese dynastic histories.

01:09:36.149 --> 01:09:40.066
(speaking in Korean)

01:09:44.323 --> 01:09:48.390
- [Juhn] And in the Shoku
Nihongi Japanese history,

01:09:48.390 --> 01:09:51.900
it states clearly that the Balhae king

01:09:51.900 --> 01:09:55.680
was attempting to recover
Goguryeo's old territory,

01:09:55.680 --> 01:09:58.258
hence the Appalachian successor's state.

01:09:58.258 --> 01:10:02.175
(speaking in Korean)

01:10:12.318 --> 01:10:15.450
- [Juhn] And in wooden tablets
and also ceramic dishes

01:10:15.450 --> 01:10:19.440
found in Japan, they refer
to the envoys from Balhae

01:10:19.440 --> 01:10:24.440
as actually envoys or diplomats
sent to, or from Goguryeo.

01:10:24.480 --> 01:10:26.670
So you can see that Balhae and Goguryeo

01:10:26.670 --> 01:10:29.573
had a very close relationship.

01:10:29.573 --> 01:10:33.490
(speaking in Korean)

01:10:36.557 --> 01:10:39.570
- [Juhn] And from the fact that
Balhae use its own era names

01:10:39.570 --> 01:10:42.129
shows you how independent they were.

01:10:42.129 --> 01:10:44.460
(speaking in Korean)

01:10:44.460 --> 01:10:46.982
- [Juhn] Joseon, for example,
cannot use its own era names.

01:10:46.982 --> 01:10:48.270
(speaking in Korean)

01:10:48.270 --> 01:10:49.920
- [Juhn] Because it
was under the influence

01:10:49.920 --> 01:10:51.372
of Chinese culture.

01:10:51.372 --> 01:10:53.386
(speaking in Korean)

01:10:53.386 --> 01:10:54.810
- [Juhn] Well, Goguryeo
and Balhae were different

01:10:54.810 --> 01:10:55.740
in this regard.

01:10:55.740 --> 01:10:56.650
- Yeah.

01:10:56.650 --> 01:11:00.567
(speaking in Korean)

01:11:02.610 --> 01:11:04.375
- [Juhn] And there are
a lot of records of them

01:11:04.375 --> 01:11:07.710
identifying themselves as the descendants

01:11:07.710 --> 01:11:09.551
from the God of heaven.

01:11:09.551 --> 01:11:13.468
(speaking in Korean)

01:11:19.860 --> 01:11:22.327
- [Juhn] So this reconstructions
of the territories

01:11:22.327 --> 01:11:25.410
of Balhae are based on
archeological evidence,

01:11:25.410 --> 01:11:28.410
and the northeastern territories,

01:11:28.410 --> 01:11:30.536
the archeological evidence
was done of course by-

01:11:30.536 --> 01:11:34.453
(speaking in Korean)

01:11:35.940 --> 01:11:37.440
- [Juhn] This is not
my field of expertise,

01:11:37.440 --> 01:11:40.279
so I've relied on the expertise of others.

01:11:40.279 --> 01:11:43.680
(speaking in Korean)

01:11:43.680 --> 01:11:46.680
- [Juhn] This is a depiction
done by the Chinese

01:11:46.680 --> 01:11:50.275
of the fort of the palace of Balhae.

01:11:50.275 --> 01:11:51.108
(speaking in Korean)

01:11:51.108 --> 01:11:52.764
- [Juhn] And that's the
front gate of that palace.

01:11:52.764 --> 01:11:53.597
(speaking in Korean)

01:11:53.597 --> 01:11:55.029
- [Juhn] And this is my last slide.

01:11:55.029 --> 01:11:58.020
(speaking in Korean)

01:11:58.020 --> 01:12:00.933
- [Juhn] He calls Balhae,
the Vikings of Asia.

01:12:01.963 --> 01:12:05.880
(speaking in Korean)

01:12:08.490 --> 01:12:10.770
- [Juhn] And in the 220 years,

01:12:10.770 --> 01:12:13.451
they sent more than 34 envoys.

01:12:13.451 --> 01:12:16.860
(speaking in Korean)

01:12:16.860 --> 01:12:21.690
- [Juhn] And 1,100 immigrants
had crossed the strait,

01:12:21.690 --> 01:12:24.003
but then they were forced to return.

01:12:24.003 --> 01:12:26.100
(speaking in Korean)

01:12:26.100 --> 01:12:30.003
- [Juhn] I've traveled this
sea, myself, many times,

01:12:31.590 --> 01:12:34.424
on a yacht, on a raft. (laughs)

01:12:34.424 --> 01:12:37.800
(speaking in Korean)

01:12:37.800 --> 01:12:39.270
- [Juhn] And I took a boat all the way up

01:12:39.270 --> 01:12:40.560
to the northern seas.

01:12:40.560 --> 01:12:41.393
(speaking in Korean)

01:12:41.393 --> 01:12:42.863
- [Juhn] For 90 days.

01:12:42.863 --> 01:12:45.360
(speaking in Korean)

01:12:45.360 --> 01:12:46.770
- [Juhn] But that sea that you see there

01:12:46.770 --> 01:12:48.759
is a very difficult sea to navigate.

01:12:48.759 --> 01:12:50.700
(speaking in Korean)

01:12:50.700 --> 01:12:51.690
- [Juhn] Especially in the winter,

01:12:51.690 --> 01:12:52.953
it's very difficult to cross.

01:12:52.953 --> 01:12:56.820
(speaking in Korean)

01:12:56.820 --> 01:12:59.190
- [Juhn] But because the Balhae people

01:12:59.190 --> 01:13:00.660
had to cross the ocean,

01:13:00.660 --> 01:13:02.799
they had to rely on the
northeasterly winds.

01:13:02.799 --> 01:13:04.440
(speaking in Korean)

01:13:04.440 --> 01:13:07.730
- [Juhn] And that's why I
call them the Vikings of Asia.

01:13:07.730 --> 01:13:12.060
(speaking in Korean)

01:13:12.060 --> 01:13:14.130
- [Juhn] There may be scholars of Japan

01:13:14.130 --> 01:13:15.856
and Japanese students here.

01:13:15.856 --> 01:13:19.773
(speaking in Korean)

01:13:27.720 --> 01:13:30.330
- [Juhn] Japanese are very
proud of their maritime history.

01:13:30.330 --> 01:13:33.450
A lot of this was reconstructed
using Japanese sources

01:13:33.450 --> 01:13:34.555
on the subject.

01:13:34.555 --> 01:13:37.920
(speaking in Korean)

01:13:37.920 --> 01:13:39.240
- Finally, the time is arrived

01:13:39.240 --> 01:13:40.320
that you were all waiting for.

01:13:40.320 --> 01:13:42.664
This is the end of the lecture,
thank you all very much.

01:13:42.664 --> 01:13:45.831
(audience applauding)

01:13:49.710 --> 01:13:52.920
- So why don't we give an
opportunity to our speakers

01:13:52.920 --> 01:13:56.070
to ask questions of each other.

01:13:56.070 --> 01:13:59.100
So Professor Westad, do
you have any question

01:13:59.100 --> 01:14:00.603
for Professor Youn?

01:14:01.685 --> 01:14:05.490
- Actually, I find the
exposition fascinating

01:14:05.490 --> 01:14:08.490
in terms of looking at the various stages

01:14:08.490 --> 01:14:12.090
of the development of for me and his.

01:14:12.090 --> 01:14:16.233
And I know that your own
expertise is connected earlier.

01:14:19.403 --> 01:14:23.320
(speaking in Korean)

01:14:29.786 --> 01:14:32.160
- So I had many questions.

01:14:32.160 --> 01:14:37.160
One question I had was
about the composition

01:14:37.170 --> 01:14:41.940
of the original inhabitants
of this larger Korean area

01:14:41.940 --> 01:14:43.948
that you outlined.

01:14:43.948 --> 01:14:47.865
(speaking in Korean)

01:14:54.590 --> 01:14:58.770
- So is there any recent genetic research

01:14:58.770 --> 01:15:00.990
that would show some of the connections

01:15:00.990 --> 01:15:04.620
between the original
inhabitants in these areas

01:15:04.620 --> 01:15:06.300
and neighboring groups?

01:15:06.300 --> 01:15:09.000
Because it's always a
big question in terms of

01:15:09.000 --> 01:15:12.060
how these people have traveled
in terms of settlement.

01:15:12.060 --> 01:15:12.893
- Right.

01:15:12.893 --> 01:15:16.440
- But also sometimes with
a little bit of luck,

01:15:16.440 --> 01:15:20.100
genetic research, DNA research,
can tell us something about

01:15:20.100 --> 01:15:23.341
how cultures travel between they took.

01:15:23.341 --> 01:15:27.258
(speaking in Korean)

01:15:34.055 --> 01:15:34.888
- Okay.

01:15:34.888 --> 01:15:37.729
(speaking in Korean)

01:15:37.729 --> 01:15:41.646
(speaking in Korean)

01:15:48.450 --> 01:15:51.220
- So scholars believe that

01:15:52.320 --> 01:15:55.380
the inhabitants of the Korean Peninsula

01:15:55.380 --> 01:15:57.390
in terms of their genetic
makeup and whatnot,

01:15:57.390 --> 01:16:00.300
had already been established
pretty much by the,

01:16:00.300 --> 01:16:03.024
somewhere between the fifth
to the seventh century CE.

01:16:03.024 --> 01:16:06.941
(speaking in Korean)

01:16:18.840 --> 01:16:20.580
- So based on textual records,

01:16:20.580 --> 01:16:22.260
we know that there was connections

01:16:22.260 --> 01:16:24.900
between Tungusic and the Mongoloid people

01:16:24.900 --> 01:16:27.180
up in the northern steppes with people

01:16:27.180 --> 01:16:29.867
who will come to inhabit
the Korean Peninsula.

01:16:29.867 --> 01:16:33.784
(speaking in Korean)

01:16:47.331 --> 01:16:49.814
- So he, okay, this is tough. (laughs)

01:16:49.814 --> 01:16:51.750
(audience laughing)

01:16:51.750 --> 01:16:54.270
So he distinguishes between a core group

01:16:54.270 --> 01:16:56.100
and then a periphery group.

01:16:56.100 --> 01:16:57.930
And the periphery group will actually

01:16:57.930 --> 01:17:01.440
establish conquest dynasties
in what are territories

01:17:01.440 --> 01:17:02.940
in China today.

01:17:02.940 --> 01:17:03.837
- Yes.

01:17:03.837 --> 01:17:07.754
(speaking in Korean)

01:17:18.060 --> 01:17:20.340
- So there is some DNA evidence to suggest

01:17:20.340 --> 01:17:24.750
that the vast majority
came from the north.

01:17:24.750 --> 01:17:26.880
There is some DNA evidence to suggest

01:17:26.880 --> 01:17:29.250
that there was an immigration
from the south as well.

01:17:29.250 --> 01:17:31.650
But Professor Youn himself
does not really think that

01:17:31.650 --> 01:17:34.320
to understand the
formation of the historical

01:17:34.320 --> 01:17:36.480
cultural community during this period

01:17:36.480 --> 01:17:39.465
that DNA is very important.

01:17:39.465 --> 01:17:44.070
(speaking in Korean)

01:17:44.070 --> 01:17:46.290
- And it really depends
on where you collect

01:17:46.290 --> 01:17:47.123
the DNA evidence.

01:17:47.123 --> 01:17:48.210
- Yes.
- So, that's why

01:17:48.210 --> 01:17:49.969
he doesn't give it too much credits.

01:17:49.969 --> 01:17:54.600
(speaking in Korean)

01:17:54.600 --> 01:17:58.620
- So the big difference
between the Chinese today

01:17:58.620 --> 01:18:01.380
and Koreans is that the
Koreans are connected,

01:18:01.380 --> 01:18:04.702
or descendants of the northern Mongoloids.

01:18:04.702 --> 01:18:08.619
(speaking in Korean)

01:18:09.690 --> 01:18:11.310
- So there are the northern Mongoloids

01:18:11.310 --> 01:18:12.988
and the southern Mongoloids.

01:18:12.988 --> 01:18:17.722
(speaking in Korean)

01:18:17.722 --> 01:18:19.070
- So Koreans are mainly descendants

01:18:19.070 --> 01:18:20.370
of the northern Mongoloids

01:18:20.370 --> 01:18:22.896
who belong to the Altaic
linguistics people.

01:18:22.896 --> 01:18:26.813
(speaking in Korean)

01:18:30.047 --> 01:18:31.440
- There are more recent research

01:18:31.440 --> 01:18:35.327
has come to criticize or deny the

01:18:35.327 --> 01:18:37.350
Altaic language group theory,

01:18:37.350 --> 01:18:40.361
but there's plenty of
evidence to suggest otherwise.

01:18:40.361 --> 01:18:44.278
(speaking in Korean)

01:18:46.740 --> 01:18:51.740
- And he believes that in our
culture, our blood, and so on.

01:18:53.178 --> 01:18:54.613
There are still-
- Like language and culture.

01:18:54.613 --> 01:18:56.160
- Right, and language.

01:18:56.160 --> 01:19:00.270
There are still strong traces
of the northern steppe culture

01:19:00.270 --> 01:19:01.103
in history.

01:19:02.520 --> 01:19:05.760
- I had just one more question if I may.

01:19:05.760 --> 01:19:10.320
I was fascinated by this
concept of the eastern,

01:19:10.320 --> 01:19:12.510
or the Eastern Asian Mediterranean.

01:19:12.510 --> 01:19:13.343
- Okay.

01:19:13.343 --> 01:19:17.610
- So this idea about how influence spread,

01:19:17.610 --> 01:19:21.753
and how goods were
exchanged in terms of this.

01:19:23.880 --> 01:19:26.310
And I'm like, you fascinated by the sea.

01:19:26.310 --> 01:19:28.693
Not surprising since I come from no one.

01:19:28.693 --> 01:19:31.004
(audience laughing)

01:19:31.004 --> 01:19:34.921
(speaking in Korean)

01:19:38.430 --> 01:19:41.330
- So I was wondering in terms of

01:19:42.174 --> 01:19:47.174
the kind of maritime vessels,
the ships that we think

01:19:47.550 --> 01:19:50.670
were being used for
these kinds of exchanges,

01:19:50.670 --> 01:19:53.550
were these different kinds of ships?

01:19:53.550 --> 01:19:55.917
Do we know anything about that at all?

01:19:55.917 --> 01:19:58.050
And was ship building technology.

01:19:58.050 --> 01:19:59.970
Because there must have
been a lot of ships

01:19:59.970 --> 01:20:01.680
going in different direction there.

01:20:01.680 --> 01:20:05.160
Was that influenced
across the various areas,

01:20:05.160 --> 01:20:07.710
or are there very different kinds of ships

01:20:07.710 --> 01:20:09.635
that contributed to this?

01:20:09.635 --> 01:20:13.552
(speaking in Korean)

01:20:19.248 --> 01:20:20.772
- If you know anything about that.

01:20:20.772 --> 01:20:23.489
(speaking in Korean)

01:20:23.489 --> 01:20:27.406
(speaking in Korean)

01:20:41.018 --> 01:20:44.935
(speaking in Korean)

01:20:46.838 --> 01:20:48.582
(speaking in Korean)

01:20:48.582 --> 01:20:52.499
(speaking in Korean)

01:20:58.899 --> 01:21:01.825
- No, no.

01:21:01.825 --> 01:21:05.742
(speaking in Korean)

01:21:20.070 --> 01:21:22.170
- So he's convinced that the ship building

01:21:22.170 --> 01:21:25.710
and the maritime technology,
China was at the top.

01:21:25.710 --> 01:21:28.350
But unfortunately, we don't have records

01:21:28.350 --> 01:21:31.972
from Korea or Japan to say more about it.

01:21:31.972 --> 01:21:35.889
(speaking in Korean)

01:21:43.020 --> 01:21:45.210
- So there's some
evidence that you can find

01:21:45.210 --> 01:21:47.430
from the Yayu in Kofun period in Japan,

01:21:47.430 --> 01:21:48.810
where you look at the murals,

01:21:48.810 --> 01:21:50.280
and you can see images of ships,

01:21:50.280 --> 01:21:53.430
and we can use those to sort of guess

01:21:53.430 --> 01:21:55.292
what the ships actually look like.

01:21:55.292 --> 01:21:59.209
(speaking in Korean)

01:22:03.159 --> 01:22:08.159
- So Professor Youn says that
the ships used in the eastern

01:22:08.880 --> 01:22:10.530
and the western seas were very different,

01:22:10.530 --> 01:22:12.270
and the ones used in the eastern sea,

01:22:12.270 --> 01:22:14.190
the one that connected
what is Russia today

01:22:14.190 --> 01:22:18.150
created in Japan, were
probably much closer in design

01:22:18.150 --> 01:22:20.116
to the Viking ships.

01:22:20.116 --> 01:22:24.330
(speaking in Korean)

01:22:24.330 --> 01:22:26.799
- And the sails themselves
have to be square.

01:22:26.799 --> 01:22:30.716
(speaking in Korean)

01:22:33.676 --> 01:22:35.610
- And he believes that
this design had to be used,

01:22:35.610 --> 01:22:37.170
because of the size of the waves,

01:22:37.170 --> 01:22:38.003
- Yes.
- and the need

01:22:38.003 --> 01:22:39.480
to rely on the north,

01:22:39.480 --> 01:22:40.403
east, and west.
- And also distance.

01:22:40.403 --> 01:22:41.236
I mean the distance.

01:22:41.236 --> 01:22:42.794
- And the distance, yes.

01:22:42.794 --> 01:22:46.711
(speaking in Korean)

01:22:48.900 --> 01:22:49.890
- So Chinese sources,

01:22:49.890 --> 01:22:52.200
they tell you exactly how
the ships were designed.

01:22:52.200 --> 01:22:54.977
So we have far more
details about that from.

01:22:54.977 --> 01:22:57.570
(speaking in Korean)

01:22:57.570 --> 01:23:01.800
- And so he doesn't
deny the sophistication

01:23:01.800 --> 01:23:03.600
of Chinese ship building technology.

01:23:04.590 --> 01:23:07.020
- Okay, since we don't have much time,

01:23:07.020 --> 01:23:11.820
why don't just jump into
just sharing with you,

01:23:11.820 --> 01:23:14.250
maybe two or three
questions from the audience.

01:23:14.250 --> 01:23:16.350
The most kind of obvious and funny one.

01:23:16.350 --> 01:23:17.190
- Okay.

01:23:17.190 --> 01:23:20.586
- Why did you feel so
ambitious to cross the waters

01:23:20.586 --> 01:23:22.710
(audience laughing)
between Korea and Japan

01:23:22.710 --> 01:23:25.879
to understand the experience yourself?

01:23:25.879 --> 01:23:29.796
(speaking in Korean)

01:23:31.897 --> 01:23:34.860
(speaking in Korean)

01:23:34.860 --> 01:23:38.213
- He believes that history
is based on action.

01:23:38.213 --> 01:23:42.130
(speaking in Korean)

01:23:44.850 --> 01:23:47.310
- You can't understand their
happiness, their sorrow,

01:23:47.310 --> 01:23:48.660
their pain, or anything

01:23:48.660 --> 01:23:50.595
unless you experience it for yourself.

01:23:50.595 --> 01:23:54.060
(speaking in Korean)

01:23:54.060 --> 01:23:55.890
- You have to experience it for yourself

01:23:55.890 --> 01:23:59.010
to just even get a glimpse
of what the experiences

01:23:59.010 --> 01:24:00.285
back in the day were like.

01:24:00.285 --> 01:24:04.202
(speaking in Korean)

01:24:05.490 --> 01:24:07.680
- And he believes that there's
a meaning of existence,

01:24:07.680 --> 01:24:11.040
not only to human beings,
to every single thing.

01:24:11.040 --> 01:24:13.170
(speaking in Korean)

01:24:13.170 --> 01:24:15.690
- And he believes that
that was the way of thought

01:24:15.690 --> 01:24:18.504
in the worldview of the
ancient Korean people as well.

01:24:18.504 --> 01:24:20.100
(speaking in Korean)

01:24:20.100 --> 01:24:21.333
- Okay, this question.

01:24:23.040 --> 01:24:25.110
I thought my handwriting was really bad,

01:24:25.110 --> 01:24:26.400
but wow,
(audience laughing)

01:24:26.400 --> 01:24:28.907
that's kinda archeological project.

01:24:28.907 --> 01:24:33.090
(speaking in Korean)

01:24:33.090 --> 01:24:35.490
- You mentioned several
cultural connections

01:24:35.490 --> 01:24:40.050
between ancient Korean
people and Altaic peoples.

01:24:40.050 --> 01:24:43.890
As far as I know, the Altaic hypothesis

01:24:43.890 --> 01:24:46.890
positing relationship
between Japanese, Korean,

01:24:46.890 --> 01:24:51.890
and various inner Asian
languages has been,

01:24:51.990 --> 01:24:53.885
finally I got the languages,

01:24:53.885 --> 01:24:55.350
(audience laughing)

01:24:55.350 --> 01:24:57.810
has been largely disproven

01:24:57.810 --> 01:25:00.685
by American and European linguists.

01:25:00.685 --> 01:25:01.518
- Okay.

01:25:01.518 --> 01:25:04.344
(speaking in Korean)

01:25:04.344 --> 01:25:05.594
- Okay, I know.

01:25:08.524 --> 01:25:09.468
Okay, I know.

01:25:09.468 --> 01:25:12.030
- So what does the category
Altaic mean to you?

01:25:12.030 --> 01:25:16.570
And does Korean language on
culture fall under the category,

01:25:19.020 --> 01:25:22.260
or is it separate but related to it?

01:25:22.260 --> 01:25:26.177
(speaking in Korean)

01:25:34.116 --> 01:25:35.670
(speaking in Korean)

01:25:35.670 --> 01:25:37.212
- I mean history.

01:25:37.212 --> 01:25:39.980
(speaking in Korean)

01:25:39.980 --> 01:25:42.883
- So my opinion is very different
from that of a linguist.

01:25:42.883 --> 01:25:47.340
(speaking in Korean)

01:25:47.340 --> 01:25:49.800
- So he is well aware of the fact

01:25:49.800 --> 01:25:52.230
that the Altaic language family theory

01:25:52.230 --> 01:25:54.533
is under attack and has been criticized.

01:25:54.533 --> 01:25:58.450
(speaking in Korean)

01:25:59.340 --> 01:26:00.900
- But he doesn't think that you can deny

01:26:00.900 --> 01:26:04.085
that there was an Altaic
linguistic cultural sphere.

01:26:04.085 --> 01:26:08.002
(speaking in Korean)

01:26:09.210 --> 01:26:12.150
- And there is strong
evidence of influence

01:26:12.150 --> 01:26:15.285
of all the northern steppes
in the Korean Peninsula.

01:26:15.285 --> 01:26:19.202
(speaking in Korean)

01:26:24.600 --> 01:26:26.970
- So he'll talk about the
traces of these influence

01:26:26.970 --> 01:26:29.637
of the northern steppes
in more detail on Friday.

01:26:29.637 --> 01:26:33.554
(speaking in Korean)

01:26:42.570 --> 01:26:47.100
- So he understands that linguists

01:26:47.100 --> 01:26:50.370
now deny the Altaic cultural sphere

01:26:50.370 --> 01:26:53.310
and linguistic sphere
theory, but as a historian,

01:26:53.310 --> 01:26:55.860
he believes that such a sphere did exist,

01:26:55.860 --> 01:26:58.410
and that it is very
useful in understanding

01:26:58.410 --> 01:27:00.390
the cultural interactions

01:27:00.390 --> 01:27:02.906
between the northern steppes
in the Korean Peninsula.

01:27:02.906 --> 01:27:03.739
(speaking in Korean)

01:27:03.739 --> 01:27:04.888
- Sorry.

01:27:04.888 --> 01:27:06.119
(speaking in Korean)

01:27:06.119 --> 01:27:06.952
(Hwansoo speaking in Korean)

01:27:06.952 --> 01:27:08.274
- Okay, yeah.

01:27:08.274 --> 01:27:12.191
(speaking in Korean)

01:27:26.882 --> 01:27:28.350
- So most people place
- Sorry.

01:27:28.350 --> 01:27:31.200
- the center of the Altaic cultural sphere

01:27:31.200 --> 01:27:32.610
in the Altaic mountain range,

01:27:32.610 --> 01:27:35.610
but he believes that it
should be placed further west

01:27:35.610 --> 01:27:38.220
above Uzbekistan and the grasslands there,

01:27:38.220 --> 01:27:40.187
and that in moves-
(speaking in Korean)

01:27:40.187 --> 01:27:41.484
- Ukraine, Ukraine.

01:27:41.484 --> 01:27:42.330
(speaking in Korean)

01:27:42.330 --> 01:27:43.898
- Ukraine, very important. (laughs)

01:27:43.898 --> 01:27:44.731
(speaking in Korean)

01:27:44.731 --> 01:27:45.564
- I'm sorry.

01:27:45.564 --> 01:27:46.770
- [Hwansoo] So we have
two remaining questions.

01:27:46.770 --> 01:27:49.320
The following question you can
just answer yes or no, okay?

01:27:49.320 --> 01:27:50.760
- Okay, I'm sorry.

01:27:50.760 --> 01:27:54.300
- Was the founder of
Gojoseon, Proto-Joseon,

01:27:54.300 --> 01:27:55.133
- Yes.

01:27:55.133 --> 01:27:59.223
- ever honored in the same
way that Gija or Tangun were?

01:28:00.071 --> 01:28:01.047
(speaking in Korean)

01:28:01.047 --> 01:28:04.170
- The Korean people, (speaking
in Korean)?

01:28:04.170 --> 01:28:05.103
- No.
- No?

01:28:06.720 --> 01:28:07.553
Okay.

01:28:08.411 --> 01:28:12.328
(speaking in Korean)

01:28:20.107 --> 01:28:21.057
(speaking in Korean)

01:28:21.057 --> 01:28:22.648
- Oh, okay, I'm sorry.

01:28:22.648 --> 01:28:26.565
(speaking in Korean)

01:28:43.980 --> 01:28:47.280
- So, then answer the question directly,

01:28:47.280 --> 01:28:51.390
but he says that the word Joseon appears

01:28:51.390 --> 01:28:53.790
in very early Chinese sources.

01:28:53.790 --> 01:28:56.580
It also appears in the
13th century Korean source.

01:28:56.580 --> 01:28:59.760
This is a very important
source known as Samguk Yusa.

01:28:59.760 --> 01:29:03.610
And there's clear evidence of
the fact that successor states

01:29:04.620 --> 01:29:06.330
clearly look to the Joseon

01:29:06.330 --> 01:29:09.120
as the state that they were succeeding.

01:29:09.120 --> 01:29:12.259
And so he believes that there
is that connection there.

01:29:12.259 --> 01:29:16.176
(speaking in Korean)

01:29:22.466 --> 01:29:26.466
- So scholars distinguish
between Tangun Joseon,

01:29:28.590 --> 01:29:30.390
Gija Joseon, and Wiman Joseon,

01:29:30.390 --> 01:29:33.990
but this is something that scholars do,

01:29:33.990 --> 01:29:36.350
but not something that really exists in.

01:29:36.350 --> 01:29:37.860
(speaking in Korean)

01:29:37.860 --> 01:29:38.957
- It's a very difficult question.

01:29:38.957 --> 01:29:39.790
- Yes.

01:29:39.790 --> 01:29:43.707
(speaking in Korean)

01:29:44.670 --> 01:29:46.800
- So he finds it very difficult himself

01:29:46.800 --> 01:29:49.838
to actually answer the question
of Gija and Wiman Joseon.

01:29:49.838 --> 01:29:52.197
(speaking in Korean)

01:29:52.197 --> 01:29:55.020
- But I do actually advance
my own personal views

01:29:55.020 --> 01:29:55.853
as historian.

01:29:55.853 --> 01:29:59.718
(speaking in Korean)

01:30:01.260 --> 01:30:05.730
- So as a scholar, he denies
the theory of Gija Joseon,

01:30:05.730 --> 01:30:09.393
but he does accept Wiman Joseon
as part of Korean history.

01:30:10.770 --> 01:30:12.060
- Okay, the time is up.

01:30:12.060 --> 01:30:14.700
I'm the person of keeping
the promise. (laughs)

01:30:14.700 --> 01:30:18.840
So those of you who wrote questions,

01:30:18.840 --> 01:30:19.920
and haven't heard the answer,

01:30:19.920 --> 01:30:23.190
you can actually come forward
and ask him in person.

01:30:23.190 --> 01:30:25.838
But thank you again,
Professor Westad, for coming,

01:30:25.838 --> 01:30:29.064
(audience applauding)
and it's a wonderful talk.

01:30:29.064 --> 01:30:30.982
And thank you, Professor Youn.

01:30:30.982 --> 01:30:33.270
This Friday at 12,

01:30:33.270 --> 01:30:35.430
we're gonna have the
second part of the talk

01:30:35.430 --> 01:30:37.080
with Korean food, okay?

01:30:37.080 --> 01:30:38.700
So please come. (laughs)

01:30:38.700 --> 01:30:40.380
Thank you so much, you guys.

01:30:40.380 --> 01:30:42.333
Thank you, have a wonderful evening.

01:30:44.479 --> 01:30:47.062
(bright music)

